[stderr] -## Domain chapters
[stderr] -
[stderr] -### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked → available → active → ready-to-complete → completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending → shown → acknowledged → complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending → active → resolving → won/lost/escaped (if authored) → rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready → selected → acted → defeated`; `Action` moves `offered → selected → validated → resolved/rejected`.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent → acquired → changed/equipped/used → depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty → writing → confirmed → loading → confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed → deleting → deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.
[stderr] -
[stderr] -**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.
[stderr] +- A quest transition, reward grant, and save snapshot are ordered: validate
[stderr] +  transition, apply reward once, then checkpoint; retrying cannot duplicate a
[stderr] +  reward.
[stderr] +- An encounter may read party, item, and quest state but changes durable state
[stderr] +  only after a resolved turn/encounter safe boundary. A crash cannot restore a
[stderr] +  half-consumed item or half-applied reward.
[stderr] +- A scene exit is available only when its authored quest predicate is true.
[stderr] +  Content validation ensures every required predicate, target, reward, and
[stderr] +  localisation key exists.
[stderr] +- Settings affect presentation/input immediately but never campaign rules.
[stderr] +  The same semantic action must be available through supported keyboard,
[stderr] +  mouse, touch, and gamepad adaptations.
[stderr] +- Save snapshots record content/schema version and are written atomically.
[stderr] +  Cache replacement and application migration happen only outside combat and
[stderr] +  only while a valid fallback save remains intact.
[stderr] +- The local offline campaign neither needs identity nor sends player state.
[stderr] +  Any later account is an optional replication boundary, not campaign truth.
[stderr]  
[stderr] -**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.
