ry | implied | A playable web/PWA game needs safe entry, pause, resume, and return from interruption. | What are we creating?; selected targets |\n| Exploration, traversal, collision, town and dungeon scenes | explicit | Exploration and exactly one town and one dungeon are named. | What are we creating? |\n| Narrative, characters, dialogue, quest text, and short ending | explicit | Story/campaign progress, readable dialogue, quests, and a short ending are direct. | Users; shared requirements; creation scope |\n| Quest state, progression, rewards, and onboarding/tutorial | explicit | Quests, progression, and onboarding are required; first session teaches battle and saving. | Required capabilities; first useful session |\n| Party combat, encounters, turn resolution, rewards, and balancing | explicit | Turn-based combat is named; party scope comes only from the functional reference. Balancing is necessary for a learnable loop. | Creation scope; functional reference |\n| Inventory, item definitions, equipment/use rules, and reward economy | explicit | Inventory is named and needs authored items/rewards. | Creation scope; shared UI requirements |\n| Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, data-loss recovery, and local persistence | explicit | Save/load, long-session checkpoints, recovery, and offline PWA save behavior are direct. | Shared requirements; PWA requirements |\n| Rendering, responsive layout, input adapter, and browser performance | explicit | Web target demands responsive layout, keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad, compatibility, and performance budget. | Responsive web requirements |\n| PWA installability, service worker, cache lifecycle, offline asset delivery, and update safety | explicit | Installability, offline assets/save behavior, and safe cache refresh are direct. | PWA requirements |\n| Accessibility: remappable/accessible controls, text, captions, visual settings, and readable UI | explicit | Accessibility and readability are named across controls and core game interfaces. | Shared requirements |\n| Localisation and language-content pipeline | explicit | Localisation is required, though languages are unknown. | Cross-platform decisions |\n| Audio/visual feedback and original art asset pipeline | explicit | Feedback and original warm hand-painted direction are requested. | Required capabilities; art direction |\n| Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery | implied | Town, dungeon, dialogue, quests, items, and level delivery require controlled authored content and validation. | Content/level delivery; explicit scope |\n| Build, release, versioning, cache/update communication, and rollback policy | implied | Safe web/PWA refresh cannot be delivered without release discipline. | PWA requirements |\n| Crash diagnostics, error boundaries, player recovery UI, and support triage | explicit | Crash reporting and player-facing error recovery are direct. | Shared requirements; required capabilities |\n| Quality engineering: gameplay, compatibility, offline/update, persistence/recovery, accessibility, and performance testing | implied | Every target and recovery promise needs validation beyond individual screens. | Per-target and shared requirements |\n| Privacy, security, consent, data minimisation, retention/deletion | explicit | Privacy is required and accounts/diagnostics may process player data. | Constraints; cross-platform decisions |\n| Account identity, authentication, remote state sync, and conflict handling | requires-decision | Named as required but no account/sync policy exists and it conflicts with offline scope. | Required capabilities; cross-platform data policy |\n| Multiplayer, player safety, moderation/reporting, and community operations | requires-decision | Named as required but inconsistent with the single-player framing and lacks mode/safety policy. | Game characteristics; required capabilities |\n| Live-service operations, status, support, incident response, and event tooling | requires-decision | Named as required but conflicts with a deliberately small offline release. | Required capabilities; creation scope |\n| Purchases, catalogue, entitlements, refunds, and compliance | requires-decision | Named as required while monetisation is deferred and no payment/platform policy is given. | Required and deferred capabilities |\n| Analytics and telemetry | deferred | Advanced analytics is explicitly later; minimum crash diagnostics remain separately required. | Later/deferred capabilities |\n| Seasonal/live events and player-created/shared content | deferred | These are explicitly listed as later scope. | Later/deferred capabilities |\n| Repository, engine/framework, backend/vendors, CI/CD, and cost model | requires-decision | The source says to determine constraints but authorizes no selection. | Constraints and non-goals |\n| Browser/device matrix, storage quota, network floor, and performance budget | requires-decision | \u201cWidely used\u201d and \u201cfeasible slow-network\u201d are not testable thresholds. | Cross-platform decisions; web requirements |\n| Age rating, parental controls, regions, store/certification, and legal policy | requires-decision | Applicable requirements are named but audience detail, regions, and commerce/service scope are unknown. | Cross-platform decisions |\n\n## Scope and dependency conclusions\n\nThe minimal offline campaign depends on the shell, exploration, narrative,\nquests/progression, combat, inventory, persistence, rendering/input,\naccessibility, original assets, content tooling, and quality disciplines. PWA\npackaging adds installability, caches, offline assets, and update safety; it\ndoes not create a separate game.\n\nAccount, multiplayer, live-service, and commerce must not become dependencies\nof the offline campaign until a human resolves their contradiction. If approved,\nthey require explicit degraded states, privacy/security rules, operational\nownership, and recovery behavior. If deferred, their interfaces and data model\nmust not expand or block the first release.\n\n## Explicit requests, safe assumptions, and approval gates\n\n| Kind | Discovery conclusion | Handling |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Explicit requests | Small 2D single-player RPG; web/PWA; exploration, town, dungeon, combat, inventory, quests, saves, settings, ending, access, feedback, recovery. | Plan as the complete offline campaign. |\n| Necessary implied capabilities | Shell/session, authored content/tooling, input/rendering, QA, release/update workflow, and balance. | Include in later contracts as enabling work, sized to the small release. |\n| Safe assumptions | Original content; shared core; durable confirmed local saves when storage permits; connected features visibly degrade; minimal-data diagnostics. | Retain as reversible guardrails only. |\n| Approval gates | Connected scope, account/sync, multiplayer/safety, commerce, stack/vendors, support matrix/budgets, content quantities, localisation, audio, and legal/store policy. | Record and obtain approval before selecting policies, services, or production quantities. |\n\n## Reference boundary\n\nThe functional reference constrains high-level party-based turn-based adventure\nscope only. It is never permission to reproduce branding, names, copy, story,\ncharacters, lore, assets, music, layouts, screen composition, maps, distinctive\nmenus, flows, or interaction choreography. Original mechanics expression,\ncontent, UI, art, and feedback must be created for this product.\n# 01 \u2014 Product brief\n\n**Canonical normalized brief:** [`../PROJECT_BRIEF.md`](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md).\n`INITIAL.md` remains the source of truth. This specification intentionally adds\nno requirements or decisions beyond that document.\n\n## Release definition\n\nDeliver an original, small, offline-capable 2D single-player fantasy RPG for a\nresponsive public web application and installable PWA. The complete path is one\ntown, one dungeon, onboarding, readable narrative/quests, tutorialized\nparty-based turn-based combat, inventory/progression, save/load/recovery,\nsettings/accessibility, and a short ending.\n\n## Requirement handling\n\n- Direct requests define the candidate first-release scope and measurable\n  outcomes in the canonical brief.\n- Safe assumptions are temporary guardrails, not service, legal, technology,\n  content, or vendor commitments.\n- Deferred roadmap work is not release acceptance work.\n- Connected services and commerce remain unresolved because they conflict with\n  the stated offline single-player product; downstream contracts need human\n  approval before selecting their architecture or policy.\n\n## Originality boundary\n\nThe named game is a functional scope reference only. No branding, copy, assets,\nlayouts, interaction choreography, or other protected expression may be copied.\nAll content, presentation, mechanics expression, and player interfaces must be\nindependently designed.\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-3/specification/02-feature-model.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n exited 1 in 0ms:\nsed: projects/mock-fantasy-quest-3/specification/02-feature-model.md: No such file or directory\n\napply patch\npatch: completed\n/Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 0f5dac970642dc368657cdcea6e66533107d9f80..5fd05892133662659f4e0eb11fd8d2756a222e5e\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,214 +1,255 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n+**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md) and the canonical\n+[project brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md). This document turns only the accepted\n+offline-game scope into a model. It records connected and commercial requests\n+as approval gates; it does not silently turn them into a first-release\n+dependency.\n \n-## Product boundary\n+## Product outcome and personas\n \n-Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n+The first release is successful when a casual or adult player can start the\n+original 2D fantasy adventure, complete an understandable first battle, save,\n+return later without losing confirmed progress, finish the single town and\n+dungeon story, and reach its short ending offline after installation or a\n+successful first asset download.\n \n-The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n-\n-## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n-\n-| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | Success signal | Relevant journeys |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n-| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n-| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n-| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n-\n-Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n+| Casual story player | When I have a short session, help me understand what to do and make visible story progress without complex controls. | Starts, wins the tutorial battle, saves, and resumes a quest. | J1\u2013J4 |\n+| Adult returning player | When I resume after interruption or a long gap, help me recover safely and understand my current objective. | Restored checkpoint is clear; no confirmed progress is silently lost. | J3, J5 |\n+| Player with access needs | When I play on my chosen device, let me read, hear, see, and control the game in a comfortable way. | Can change supported text, caption, visual, and control options before or during play. | J1, J6 |\n+| Offline PWA player | When connectivity changes or an update is available, let me keep playing the installed game without corrupting saves. | Cached release runs offline; update waits for a safe boundary. | J1, J5, J7 |\n \n ## Core journeys\n \n-| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| ID | Journey and outcome | Normal path | Empty / invalid / denied / offline / conflict / recovery / deletion handling |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n-| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n-| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n-| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n-| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n+| J1 | Start, orient, and configure: player reaches the town and knows the tutorial goal. | Open web game or installed PWA \u2192 choose new game \u2192 optional settings \u2192 readable dialogue and input hints \u2192 town. | No prior save shows New Game; unavailable gamepad falls back to listed keyboard/mouse/touch controls; unsupported input shows a non-blocking message. Offline launch is allowed only when the current assets are cached; otherwise explain that an initial download is needed. Declining optional diagnostics does not block play. |\n+| J2 | Learn and win first combat: player understands the turn loop. | Accept first encounter \u2192 choose a legal party action \u2192 resolve enemy turn \u2192 win \u2192 receive reward and quest update. | Empty inventory/action lists show explanation; invalid/stale target or unavailable item is rejected without consuming a turn; defeated party reloads the latest safe checkpoint and explains the consequence. No network permission is needed. |\n+| J3 | Explore and advance the campaign: player finishes town, dungeon, and ending. | Talk to characters \u2192 accept quest \u2192 traverse town/dungeon \u2192 resolve required encounter and objective \u2192 claim reward \u2192 see ending. | Locked exits state the missing quest condition; exhausted dialogue has a safe closing line; missing authored reference fails content validation and shows recoverable error rather than breaking state; optional reward absence never blocks completion. |\n+| J4 | Manage items and progression: player can use earned resources correctly. | Open inventory \u2192 inspect item \u2192 use/equip only where legal \u2192 see immediate feedback \u2192 close back to play. | Empty inventory has an explicit empty state; invalid use, full/limited capacity (if authored), or ineligible target preserves inventory; quest items cannot be discarded if that would make a required path impossible. Deletion/discard is not a first-release capability unless an authored safe rule is later approved. |\n+| J5 | Save, load, and recover: player can leave and return safely. | Manual save or checkpoint completes atomically \u2192 resume chooses latest valid save \u2192 state restores at a safe scene boundary. | No save slot shows explanation; corrupt/incompatible candidate is quarantined, never overwritten, and falls back to the last valid checkpoint or new game; storage denial/quota exposes retry and export/support guidance where feasible; interrupted write keeps prior confirmed save. Local cache and any future remote copy conflict resolve only with explicit player choice; remote sync is not first release. Deleting a save requires confirmation and removes only that selected local save. |\n+| J6 | Adjust accessibility and feedback: player can make the game usable. | Open settings \u2192 preview/change text, captions, visual and input options \u2192 apply \u2192 preferences persist. | Unsupported option/device is labelled unavailable; reset asks confirmation; settings storage failure leaves current-session setting active and explains it may not persist. No permission denial is required for these settings. |\n+| J7 | Install, update, and recover from a fault: player retains a coherent playable version. | Install when browser allows \u2192 cache approved release \u2192 play offline \u2192 accept update at launch/menu after save boundary. | Installation prompt may be unavailable/declined without blocking web play; offline uncached assets state the requirement; cache refresh failure retains known-good cache; crash/error boundary offers retry, return to title, and safe recovery. Diagnostics are minimal and consent-gated where required; a player can decline or delete local diagnostic data without losing play. |\n \n-## Domain vocabulary\n+## Domain vocabulary and state ownership\n \n-| Term | Meaning and rule |\n+| Term | Meaning / owner |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n-| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n-| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n-| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n-| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n-| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n-| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n-| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n-| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n-| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n-| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n-| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n+| Campaign | The fixed first-release sequence from new game through ending; owns completion state. |\n+| Scene | One authored playable location: town or dungeon; owns exits, encounter zones, and presentation references. |\n+| Quest | Authored objective and state machine that gates campaign progress and rewards. |\n+| Party | Player-controlled combat group; owns members' current combat-ready values and progression. Exact party size is an authoring decision. |\n+| Encounter | A combat instance created from an authored encounter definition; never persists mid-turn. |\n+| Item | Authored definition; an inventory stack/reference is the player-owned instance/count. |\n+| Save snapshot | Versioned, validated, atomic representation of durable player state at a safe boundary. |\n+| Checkpoint | Authored or system-selected safe snapshot point; it is not a promise to restore arbitrary moment-to-moment state. |\n+| Content package | Versioned original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, translations, and audiovisual assets delivered together. |\n \n-## System relationship map and invariants\n+## System relationship map\n \n ```text\n-Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n-                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n-Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n-Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n-Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n-All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n-PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n-Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n-Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n-accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n+Content package \u2500\u2500validates/defines\u2500\u2500> Scene \u2500\u2500starts\u2500\u2500> Encounter\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502                  \u2502\n+      \u251c\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Dialogue \u2500\u2500updates\u2500\u2500> Quest <\u2500\u2500rewards\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502       \u2502\n+      \u2514\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Items \u2500\u2500held by\u2500\u2500> Inventory \u2502\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500drives\u2500\u2500> Game shell \u2500\u2500> exploration / UI\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Quest + inventory + party + scene \u2500\u2500snapshot\u2500\u2500> Save / checkpoint\n+Save version <\u2500\u2500compatible-with\u2500\u2500 Content package / PWA release\n+PWA cache + release workflow \u2500\u2500protect\u2500\u2500> runnable assets and save boundary\n+Error recovery + minimal diagnostics \u2500\u2500observe\u2500\u2500> shell, persistence, cache\n ```\n \n-Cross-domain invariants:\n+**Cross-domain invariants and lifecycle boundaries**\n \n-1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n-2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n-3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n-4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n-5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n-6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n-\n-Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n-\n-## Domain chapters\n-\n-### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n-\n-### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n-\n-### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n-\n-### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n-\n-### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n-\n-### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n-\n-### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n-\n-### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+- A quest transition, reward grant, and save snapshot are ordered: validate\n+  transition, apply reward once, then checkpoint; retrying cannot duplicate a\n+  reward.\n+- An encounter may read party, item, and quest state but changes durable state\n+  only after a resolved turn/encounter safe boundary. A crash cannot restore a\n+  half-consumed item or half-applied reward.\n+- A scene exit is available only when its authored quest predicate is true.\n+  Content validation ensures every required predicate, target, reward, and\n+  localisation key exists.\n+- Settings affect presentation/input immediately but never campaign rules.\n+  The same semantic action must be available through supported keyboard,\n+  mouse, touch, and gamepad adaptations.\n+- Save snapshots record content/schema version and are written atomically.\n+  Cache replacement and application migration happen only outside combat and\n+  only while a valid fallback save remains intact.\n+- The local offline campaign neither needs identity nor sends player state.\n+  Any later account is an optional replication boundary, not campaign truth.\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+## Accepted domain chapters\n \n-### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+### 1. Game shell, session, input, and responsive presentation\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Session` moves `launching \u2192 title \u2192 new-or-resume \u2192\n+playing \u2194 paused \u2192 ending \u2192 title`; `InputAction` is mapped per supported input\n+method; `ViewportProfile` selects responsive layout. Authoring inputs are title\n+copy, control hints, menu routes, and UI layouts. Rules: pause blocks world and\n+combat advancement; focus loss pauses or reaches a safe state; UI action labels\n+remain readable and semantic across screen sizes. Dependencies are accessibility,\n+rendering, persistence, and content. Outcome is an understandable, controllable\n+start and return path. Edge cases are no existing save, focus loss, rotation,\n+unavailable gamepad, unsupported browser feature, and input remap conflict.\n+**First-release boundary:** web/PWA keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad\n+adaptation; one shared game core. No native-client shell or arbitrary controller\n+configuration guarantee.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+### 2. Exploration, town, dungeon, and campaign content\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Scene` is `unloaded \u2192 entered \u2192 active \u2192 exited`;\n+`Exit` is `locked | available | used`; `Interactable` is `available \u2192 used` or\n+reusable as authored. Inputs are original tile/layout data, collision shapes,\n+spawn points, NPCs, exits, encounter zones, dialogue, and asset references.\n+Rules: exactly one town and one dungeon form the first campaign; collision and\n+gates must leave a completable route. Dependencies are narrative, quests,\n+combat, rendering, and saves. Outcome is readable navigation to the ending.\n+Edge cases: blocked exit, empty interaction, absent optional item, and invalid\n+content reference. **Boundary:** no extra regions, procedural worlds, or shared\n+levels.\n \n-### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+### 3. Narrative, quests, onboarding, and ending\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Dialogue` is `available \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged`;\n+`Quest` is `unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 accepted \u2192 objective-complete \u2192\n+claimed \u2192 completed` (or `failed` only where authored); `TutorialStep` is\n+`pending \u2192 presented \u2192 understood`; `Campaign` is `not-started \u2192 active \u2192\n+completed`. Inputs are original characters, text, localisation keys, objective\n+predicates, rewards, and ending sequence. Rules: tutorial teaches a battle and\n+saving before unrestricted progression; required quests cannot be abandoned;\n+completion is idempotent. Dependencies are scenes, combat, inventory, UI, and\n+saves. Outcomes are clear objectives and a short finish. Edge cases: repeated\n+NPC talk, dialogue after completion, missing translation, and an invalid quest\n+predicate. **Boundary:** one authored story path; no branching campaign,\n+seasonal narrative, or user-authored stories.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+### 4. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Encounter` is `pending \u2192 active \u2192 player-turn \u2192\n+resolution \u2192 won | lost | escaped-if-authored`; `Combatant` is `ready \u2192 acting\n+\u2192 resolved \u2192 defeated`; `Reward` is `pending \u2192 granted`. Inputs are party and\n+enemy definitions, actions, targeting, encounter placement, reward tables, and\n+balance values. Rules: only legal actions/targets resolve; turn order is visible;\n+reward grants once; defeat returns to a safe checkpoint. Dependencies are party,\n+inventory, quest transitions, audiovisual feedback, and saves. Outcome is a\n+learnable turn-based first battle and reliable progress. Edge cases: no legal\n+target, empty item list, invalid action request, pause/focus loss, crash during\n+resolution, and stale UI selection. **Boundary:** authored encounters only; no\n+multiplayer, ranked modes, live balance changes, or mid-combat save guarantee.\n \n-### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+### 5. Inventory, party progression, and economy\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `InventoryEntry` is `absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 usable |\n+equipped \u2192 consumed-if-applicable`; `PartyMember` progresses through authored\n+states; `RewardGrant` is `unclaimed \u2192 applied`. Inputs are item definitions,\n+descriptions, icons, use/equip restrictions, and rewards. Rules: use validates\n+target and eligibility before mutation; required quest items remain protected;\n+duplicate grants are ignored by stable reward ID. Dependencies are combat,\n+quests, UI, content validation, and saves. Outcome is meaningful reward use\n+without dead ends. Edge cases: empty inventory, ineligible target, capacity\n+limit if selected, duplicate reward, and missing item definition. **Boundary:**\n+small authored set; no crafting, trading, loot boxes, purchases, or destructive\n+discard flow without approval.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n+### 6. Save, recovery, and offline persistence\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `SaveSlot` is `empty \u2192 writing \u2192 valid \u2192 loading` or\n+`quarantined`; `Checkpoint` is `eligible \u2192 committed`; recovery is `detect \u2192\n+preserve \u2192 offer-fallback \u2192 restored | new-game`. Inputs are schema version,\n+campaign/quest/party/inventory/settings state, checkpoint triggers, and\n+migration rules. Rules: validate before replace; retain last known-good data;\n+never overwrite corruption; loading restores only safe scene boundaries; delete\n+requires explicit confirmation. Dependencies are every durable domain, PWA\n+storage, error UX, and release versioning. Outcome is confidence to stop and\n+resume. Edge cases: quota/permission denial, malformed data, interrupted write,\n+old schema, unavailable local storage, offline launch, and later remote conflict.\n+**Boundary:** local durable saves and recovery; remote accounts, sync, cross-\n+device merge, and cloud deletion policy await approval.\n \n-### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n+### 7. Accessibility, localisation, and audiovisual feedback\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Preference` is `default \u2192 previewed \u2192 applied \u2192\n+persisted`; `LocalePack` is `validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active`; `FeedbackEvent`\n+is emitted by action/scene/combat state. Inputs are accessible labels, text\n+styles, captions, visual settings, translations, original art/audio, and event\n+bindings. Rules: settings are understandable and reversible; captions/text do\n+not convey essential information only through sound/colour; missing translation\n+falls back to the approved base language and is logged for content repair.\n+Dependencies are UI, content packaging, input, and storage. Outcome is readable,\n+controllable play with clear feedback. Edge cases: unsupported preference,\n+storage failure, missing locale key, muted audio, contrast conflict, and narrow\n+viewport. **Boundary:** the settings categories named in the brief and a\n+language-content pipeline; exact languages, remapping depth, ratings, and audio\n+production scope remain decisions.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n+### 8. PWA delivery, release safety, diagnostics, and quality operations\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Release` is `built \u2192 validated \u2192 published \u2192 cached \u2192\n+superseded`; `Cache` is `installing \u2192 ready \u2192 retained | replaced`; `Fault` is\n+`caught \u2192 player-recovered \u2192 optionally-reported`. Inputs are asset manifests,\n+content/save compatibility declarations, service-worker policy, error messages,\n+minimal diagnostic schema, and test matrices. Rules: an update activates at a\n+title/menu safe boundary after a confirmed save; failed refresh retains a\n+known-good cache; diagnostics minimise data and respect consent; supported\n+browser/device/network and performance thresholds must be agreed before release\n+acceptance. Dependencies are all packaged content, persistence, shell, privacy,\n+and quality checks. Outcome is safe installation, offline continuation, update,\n+and error recovery. Edge cases: declined install, first offline visit, stale\n+cache, asset fetch failure, crash loop, diagnostics denial/deletion, and storage\n+pressure. **Boundary:** installable web/PWA and minimal crash recovery only; no\n+live event operation, account support desk, analytics programme, or paid-service\n+SLO until approved.\n \n-## Prioritised feature catalogue\n+## Capability parity and intentional differences\n \n-| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n+| Capability | Responsive web | Installed PWA | Shared rule |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n+| Campaign, combat, inventory, quests, settings, saves | Required | Required | Same content, rules, save schema, and accessible semantic actions. |\n+| Input | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad | Same where device/browser exposes it | Input adapters map to one action vocabulary. |\n+| Responsive presentation | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Layout adapts; no gameplay advantage by surface. |\n+| Offline play/assets | May work once cached | Primary expected offline route once cached | First visit needs assets; saves remain local and recoverable. |\n+| Installation | Browser page can offer it where supported | Installable experience | Declining/unavailable install never blocks web play. |\n+| Updates | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Never swap content/version during unresolved combat or save write. |\n \n-## Cross-target contract\n+## Prioritised feature catalogue and slices\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n+| Priority | Feature | User outcome / journey | Slice |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n-| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n-| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n-| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n-| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n-| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness proof\n+| P0 | Shell, title/new game, pause, responsive input/presentation | Start and control the game (J1). | First release |\n+| P0 | One town, one dungeon, original content packaging and validation | Traverse one completable campaign (J3). | First release |\n+| P0 | Dialogue, quest state, onboarding, short ending | Understand and complete story progress (J1\u2013J3). | First release |\n+| P0 | Party turn-based combat, tutorial encounter, rewards | Finish first battle and understand the loop (J2). | First release |\n+| P0 | Inventory/progression UI and safe item rules | Use earned rewards without dead ends (J4). | First release |\n+| P0 | Atomic local save/load, checkpoints, corruption recovery, save deletion | Stop, resume, and recover confirmed progress (J5). | First release |\n+| P0 | Settings: accessible controls/text/captions/visuals; localisation pipeline | Play comfortably and read core UI (J1, J6). | First release |\n+| P0 | Web/PWA caching, install path, update safety, error recovery | Keep a stable offline-capable version (J7). | First release |\n+| P0 | Original art/audio feedback and quality validation | Receive clear, original feedback on supported targets (J2, J6). | First release |\n+| P1 | Additional locales, refined remapping, content expansion | Broader comfort and longer campaign. | Later, after languages/content scope is approved |\n+| P1 | Optional account-linked backup/sync | Recover across devices with explicit conflict choices. | Later, approval required |\n+| P1 | Multiplayer, player safety, moderation, support and live operations | Play safely with others. | Later, approval required; not implied by single-player game |\n+| P2 | Live events/seasonal content; player-created/shared content | Receive or create continuing content. | Deferred |\n+| P2 | Advanced analytics/monetisation, purchases, catalogue, entitlements/refunds | Optional commercial/personalised experience. | Deferred; commerce approval required |\n \n-J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n+The P0 set deliberately forms a complete useful journey: J1 starts and teaches,\n+J2 proves the core loop, J3 reaches the ending, J4 supports rewards, J5 makes\n+progress durable, and J6\u2013J7 keep it usable and safe on both targets. None\n+depends on identity, remote services, multiplayer, commerce, or deferred\n+content.\n \n-## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n+## Approval gates and unanswered questions\n \n-Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n+- Select technology, repository, engine/framework, vendors, CI/CD, cost model,\n+  browser/device/network matrix, storage quota, and performance budget.\n+- Decide whether the apparently contradictory account, multiplayer, safety,\n+  live-service, and commerce requirements are cancelled, later scope, or a\n+  separately funded product. Define ownership, consent, retention/deletion,\n+  support, and degraded modes before implementation.\n+- Set exact party/content quantities, languages, audio scope, accessibility\n+  configuration depth, age/parental/legal regions, and certification/store\n+  obligations. These are not inferred from the functional reference.\n\nhook: PostToolUse\nhook: PostToolUse Failed\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 0f5dac970642dc368657cdcea6e66533107d9f80..5fd05892133662659f4e0eb11fd8d2756a222e5e\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,214 +1,255 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n+**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md) and the canonical\n+[project brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md). This document turns only the accepted\n+offline-game scope into a model. It records connected and commercial requests\n+as approval gates; it does not silently turn them into a first-release\n+dependency.\n \n-## Product boundary\n+## Product outcome and personas\n \n-Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n+The first release is successful when a casual or adult player can start the\n+original 2D fantasy adventure, complete an understandable first battle, save,\n+return later without losing confirmed progress, finish the single town and\n+dungeon story, and reach its short ending offline after installation or a\n+successful first asset download.\n \n-The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n-\n-## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n-\n-| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | Success signal | Relevant journeys |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n-| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n-| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n-| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n-\n-Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n+| Casual story player | When I have a short session, help me understand what to do and make visible story progress without complex controls. | Starts, wins the tutorial battle, saves, and resumes a quest. | J1\u2013J4 |\n+| Adult returning player | When I resume after interruption or a long gap, help me recover safely and understand my current objective. | Restored checkpoint is clear; no confirmed progress is silently lost. | J3, J5 |\n+| Player with access needs | When I play on my chosen device, let me read, hear, see, and control the game in a comfortable way. | Can change supported text, caption, visual, and control options before or during play. | J1, J6 |\n+| Offline PWA player | When connectivity changes or an update is available, let me keep playing the installed game without corrupting saves. | Cached release runs offline; update waits for a safe boundary. | J1, J5, J7 |\n \n ## Core journeys\n \n-| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| ID | Journey and outcome | Normal path | Empty / invalid / denied / offline / conflict / recovery / deletion handling |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n-| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n-| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n-| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n-| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n+| J1 | Start, orient, and configure: player reaches the town and knows the tutorial goal. | Open web game or installed PWA \u2192 choose new game \u2192 optional settings \u2192 readable dialogue and input hints \u2192 town. | No prior save shows New Game; unavailable gamepad falls back to listed keyboard/mouse/touch controls; unsupported input shows a non-blocking message. Offline launch is allowed only when the current assets are cached; otherwise explain that an initial download is needed. Declining optional diagnostics does not block play. |\n+| J2 | Learn and win first combat: player understands the turn loop. | Accept first encounter \u2192 choose a legal party action \u2192 resolve enemy turn \u2192 win \u2192 receive reward and quest update. | Empty inventory/action lists show explanation; invalid/stale target or unavailable item is rejected without consuming a turn; defeated party reloads the latest safe checkpoint and explains the consequence. No network permission is needed. |\n+| J3 | Explore and advance the campaign: player finishes town, dungeon, and ending. | Talk to characters \u2192 accept quest \u2192 traverse town/dungeon \u2192 resolve required encounter and objective \u2192 claim reward \u2192 see ending. | Locked exits state the missing quest condition; exhausted dialogue has a safe closing line; missing authored reference fails content validation and shows recoverable error rather than breaking state; optional reward absence never blocks completion. |\n+| J4 | Manage items and progression: player can use earned resources correctly. | Open inventory \u2192 inspect item \u2192 use/equip only where legal \u2192 see immediate feedback \u2192 close back to play. | Empty inventory has an explicit empty state; invalid use, full/limited capacity (if authored), or ineligible target preserves inventory; quest items cannot be discarded if that would make a required path impossible. Deletion/discard is not a first-release capability unless an authored safe rule is later approved. |\n+| J5 | Save, load, and recover: player can leave and return safely. | Manual save or checkpoint completes atomically \u2192 resume chooses latest valid save \u2192 state restores at a safe scene boundary. | No save slot shows explanation; corrupt/incompatible candidate is quarantined, never overwritten, and falls back to the last valid checkpoint or new game; storage denial/quota exposes retry and export/support guidance where feasible; interrupted write keeps prior confirmed save. Local cache and any future remote copy conflict resolve only with explicit player choice; remote sync is not first release. Deleting a save requires confirmation and removes only that selected local save. |\n+| J6 | Adjust accessibility and feedback: player can make the game usable. | Open settings \u2192 preview/change text, captions, visual and input options \u2192 apply \u2192 preferences persist. | Unsupported option/device is labelled unavailable; reset asks confirmation; settings storage failure leaves current-session setting active and explains it may not persist. No permission denial is required for these settings. |\n+| J7 | Install, update, and recover from a fault: player retains a coherent playable version. | Install when browser allows \u2192 cache approved release \u2192 play offline \u2192 accept update at launch/menu after save boundary. | Installation prompt may be unavailable/declined without blocking web play; offline uncached assets state the requirement; cache refresh failure retains known-good cache; crash/error boundary offers retry, return to title, and safe recovery. Diagnostics are minimal and consent-gated where required; a player can decline or delete local diagnostic data without losing play. |\n \n-## Domain vocabulary\n+## Domain vocabulary and state ownership\n \n-| Term | Meaning and rule |\n+| Term | Meaning / owner |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n-| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n-| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n-| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n-| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n-| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n-| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n-| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n-| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n-| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n-| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n-| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n+| Campaign | The fixed first-release sequence from new game through ending; owns completion state. |\n+| Scene | One authored playable location: town or dungeon; owns exits, encounter zones, and presentation references. |\n+| Quest | Authored objective and state machine that gates campaign progress and rewards. |\n+| Party | Player-controlled combat group; owns members' current combat-ready values and progression. Exact party size is an authoring decision. |\n+| Encounter | A combat instance created from an authored encounter definition; never persists mid-turn. |\n+| Item | Authored definition; an inventory stack/reference is the player-owned instance/count. |\n+| Save snapshot | Versioned, validated, atomic representation of durable player state at a safe boundary. |\n+| Checkpoint | Authored or system-selected safe snapshot point; it is not a promise to restore arbitrary moment-to-moment state. |\n+| Content package | Versioned original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, translations, and audiovisual assets delivered together. |\n \n-## System relationship map and invariants\n+## System relationship map\n \n ```text\n-Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n-                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n-Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n-Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n-Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n-All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n-PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n-Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n-Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n-accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n+Content package \u2500\u2500validates/defines\u2500\u2500> Scene \u2500\u2500starts\u2500\u2500> Encounter\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502                  \u2502\n+      \u251c\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Dialogue \u2500\u2500updates\u2500\u2500> Quest <\u2500\u2500rewards\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502       \u2502\n+      \u2514\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Items \u2500\u2500held by\u2500\u2500> Inventory \u2502\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500drives\u2500\u2500> Game shell \u2500\u2500> exploration / UI\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Quest + inventory + party + scene \u2500\u2500snapshot\u2500\u2500> Save / checkpoint\n+Save version <\u2500\u2500compatible-with\u2500\u2500 Content package / PWA release\n+PWA cache + release workflow \u2500\u2500protect\u2500\u2500> runnable assets and save boundary\n+Error recovery + minimal diagnostics \u2500\u2500observe\u2500\u2500> shell, persistence, cache\n ```\n \n-Cross-domain invariants:\n+**Cross-domain invariants and lifecycle boundaries**\n \n-1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n-2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n-3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n-4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n-5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n-6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n-\n-Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n-\n-## Domain chapters\n-\n-### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n-\n-### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n-\n-### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n-\n-### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n-\n-### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n-\n-### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n-\n-### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n-\n-### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+- A quest transition, reward grant, and save snapshot are ordered: validate\n+  transition, apply reward once, then checkpoint; retrying cannot duplicate a\n+  reward.\n+- An encounter may read party, item, and quest state but changes durable state\n+  only after a resolved turn/encounter safe boundary. A crash cannot restore a\n+  half-consumed item or half-applied reward.\n+- A scene exit is available only when its authored quest predicate is true.\n+  Content validation ensures every required predicate, target, reward, and\n+  localisation key exists.\n+- Settings affect presentation/input immediately but never campaign rules.\n+  The same semantic action must be available through supported keyboard,\n+  mouse, touch, and gamepad adaptations.\n+- Save snapshots record content/schema version and are written atomically.\n+  Cache replacement and application migration happen only outside combat and\n+  only while a valid fallback save remains intact.\n+- The local offline campaign neither needs identity nor sends player state.\n+  Any later account is an optional replication boundary, not campaign truth.\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+## Accepted domain chapters\n \n-### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+### 1. Game shell, session, input, and responsive presentation\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Session` moves `launching \u2192 title \u2192 new-or-resume \u2192\n+playing \u2194 paused \u2192 ending \u2192 title`; `InputAction` is mapped per supported input\n+method; `ViewportProfile` selects responsive layout. Authoring inputs are title\n+copy, control hints, menu routes, and UI layouts. Rules: pause blocks world and\n+combat advancement; focus loss pauses or reaches a safe state; UI action labels\n+remain readable and semantic across screen sizes. Dependencies are accessibility,\n+rendering, persistence, and content. Outcome is an understandable, controllable\n+start and return path. Edge cases are no existing save, focus loss, rotation,\n+unavailable gamepad, unsupported browser feature, and input remap conflict.\n+**First-release boundary:** web/PWA keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad\n+adaptation; one shared game core. No native-client shell or arbitrary controller\n+configuration guarantee.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+### 2. Exploration, town, dungeon, and campaign content\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Scene` is `unloaded \u2192 entered \u2192 active \u2192 exited`;\n+`Exit` is `locked | available | used`; `Interactable` is `available \u2192 used` or\n+reusable as authored. Inputs are original tile/layout data, collision shapes,\n+spawn points, NPCs, exits, encounter zones, dialogue, and asset references.\n+Rules: exactly one town and one dungeon form the first campaign; collision and\n+gates must leave a completable route. Dependencies are narrative, quests,\n+combat, rendering, and saves. Outcome is readable navigation to the ending.\n+Edge cases: blocked exit, empty interaction, absent optional item, and invalid\n+content reference. **Boundary:** no extra regions, procedural worlds, or shared\n+levels.\n \n-### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+### 3. Narrative, quests, onboarding, and ending\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Dialogue` is `available \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged`;\n+`Quest` is `unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 accepted \u2192 objective-complete \u2192\n+claimed \u2192 completed` (or `failed` only where authored); `TutorialStep` is\n+`pending \u2192 presented \u2192 understood`; `Campaign` is `not-started \u2192 active \u2192\n+completed`. Inputs are original characters, text, localisation keys, objective\n+predicates, rewards, and ending sequence. Rules: tutorial teaches a battle and\n+saving before unrestricted progression; required quests cannot be abandoned;\n+completion is idempotent. Dependencies are scenes, combat, inventory, UI, and\n+saves. Outcomes are clear objectives and a short finish. Edge cases: repeated\n+NPC talk, dialogue after completion, missing translation, and an invalid quest\n+predicate. **Boundary:** one authored story path; no branching campaign,\n+seasonal narrative, or user-authored stories.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+### 4. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Encounter` is `pending \u2192 active \u2192 player-turn \u2192\n+resolution \u2192 won | lost | escaped-if-authored`; `Combatant` is `ready \u2192 acting\n+\u2192 resolved \u2192 defeated`; `Reward` is `pending \u2192 granted`. Inputs are party and\n+enemy definitions, actions, targeting, encounter placement, reward tables, and\n+balance values. Rules: only legal actions/targets resolve; turn order is visible;\n+reward grants once; defeat returns to a safe checkpoint. Dependencies are party,\n+inventory, quest transitions, audiovisual feedback, and saves. Outcome is a\n+learnable turn-based first battle and reliable progress. Edge cases: no legal\n+target, empty item list, invalid action request, pause/focus loss, crash during\n+resolution, and stale UI selection. **Boundary:** authored encounters only; no\n+multiplayer, ranked modes, live balance changes, or mid-combat save guarantee.\n \n-### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+### 5. Inventory, party progression, and economy\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `InventoryEntry` is `absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 usable |\n+equipped \u2192 consumed-if-applicable`; `PartyMember` progresses through authored\n+states; `RewardGrant` is `unclaimed \u2192 applied`. Inputs are item definitions,\n+descriptions, icons, use/equip restrictions, and rewards. Rules: use validates\n+target and eligibility before mutation; required quest items remain protected;\n+duplicate grants are ignored by stable reward ID. Dependencies are combat,\n+quests, UI, content validation, and saves. Outcome is meaningful reward use\n+without dead ends. Edge cases: empty inventory, ineligible target, capacity\n+limit if selected, duplicate reward, and missing item definition. **Boundary:**\n+small authored set; no crafting, trading, loot boxes, purchases, or destructive\n+discard flow without approval.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n+### 6. Save, recovery, and offline persistence\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `SaveSlot` is `empty \u2192 writing \u2192 valid \u2192 loading` or\n+`quarantined`; `Checkpoint` is `eligible \u2192 committed`; recovery is `detect \u2192\n+preserve \u2192 offer-fallback \u2192 restored | new-game`. Inputs are schema version,\n+campaign/quest/party/inventory/settings state, checkpoint triggers, and\n+migration rules. Rules: validate before replace; retain last known-good data;\n+never overwrite corruption; loading restores only safe scene boundaries; delete\n+requires explicit confirmation. Dependencies are every durable domain, PWA\n+storage, error UX, and release versioning. Outcome is confidence to stop and\n+resume. Edge cases: quota/permission denial, malformed data, interrupted write,\n+old schema, unavailable local storage, offline launch, and later remote conflict.\n+**Boundary:** local durable saves and recovery; remote accounts, sync, cross-\n+device merge, and cloud deletion policy await approval.\n \n-### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n+### 7. Accessibility, localisation, and audiovisual feedback\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Preference` is `default \u2192 previewed \u2192 applied \u2192\n+persisted`; `LocalePack` is `validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active`; `FeedbackEvent`\n+is emitted by action/scene/combat state. Inputs are accessible labels, text\n+styles, captions, visual settings, translations, original art/audio, and event\n+bindings. Rules: settings are understandable and reversible; captions/text do\n+not convey essential information only through sound/colour; missing translation\n+falls back to the approved base language and is logged for content repair.\n+Dependencies are UI, content packaging, input, and storage. Outcome is readable,\n+controllable play with clear feedback. Edge cases: unsupported preference,\n+storage failure, missing locale key, muted audio, contrast conflict, and narrow\n+viewport. **Boundary:** the settings categories named in the brief and a\n+language-content pipeline; exact languages, remapping depth, ratings, and audio\n+production scope remain decisions.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n+### 8. PWA delivery, release safety, diagnostics, and quality operations\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Release` is `built \u2192 validated \u2192 published \u2192 cached \u2192\n+superseded`; `Cache` is `installing \u2192 ready \u2192 retained | replaced`; `Fault` is\n+`caught \u2192 player-recovered \u2192 optionally-reported`. Inputs are asset manifests,\n+content/save compatibility declarations, service-worker policy, error messages,\n+minimal diagnostic schema, and test matrices. Rules: an update activates at a\n+title/menu safe boundary after a confirmed save; failed refresh retains a\n+known-good cache; diagnostics minimise data and respect consent; supported\n+browser/device/network and performance thresholds must be agreed before release\n+acceptance. Dependencies are all packaged content, persistence, shell, privacy,\n+and quality checks. Outcome is safe installation, offline continuation, update,\n+and error recovery. Edge cases: declined install, first offline visit, stale\n+cache, asset fetch failure, crash loop, diagnostics denial/deletion, and storage\n+pressure. **Boundary:** installable web/PWA and minimal crash recovery only; no\n+live event operation, account support desk, analytics programme, or paid-service\n+SLO until approved.\n \n-## Prioritised feature catalogue\n+## Capability parity and intentional differences\n \n-| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n+| Capability | Responsive web | Installed PWA | Shared rule |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n+| Campaign, combat, inventory, quests, settings, saves | Required | Required | Same content, rules, save schema, and accessible semantic actions. |\n+| Input | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad | Same where device/browser exposes it | Input adapters map to one action vocabulary. |\n+| Responsive presentation | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Layout adapts; no gameplay advantage by surface. |\n+| Offline play/assets | May work once cached | Primary expected offline route once cached | First visit needs assets; saves remain local and recoverable. |\n+| Installation | Browser page can offer it where supported | Installable experience | Declining/unavailable install never blocks web play. |\n+| Updates | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Never swap content/version during unresolved combat or save write. |\n \n-## Cross-target contract\n+## Prioritised feature catalogue and slices\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n+| Priority | Feature | User outcome / journey | Slice |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n-| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n-| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n-| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n-| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n-| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness proof\n+| P0 | Shell, title/new game, pause, responsive input/presentation | Start and control the game (J1). | First release |\n+| P0 | One town, one dungeon, original content packaging and validation | Traverse one completable campaign (J3). | First release |\n+| P0 | Dialogue, quest state, onboarding, short ending | Understand and complete story progress (J1\u2013J3). | First release |\n+| P0 | Party turn-based combat, tutorial encounter, rewards | Finish first battle and understand the loop (J2). | First release |\n+| P0 | Inventory/progression UI and safe item rules | Use earned rewards without dead ends (J4). | First release |\n+| P0 | Atomic local save/load, checkpoints, corruption recovery, save deletion | Stop, resume, and recover confirmed progress (J5). | First release |\n+| P0 | Settings: accessible controls/text/captions/visuals; localisation pipeline | Play comfortably and read core UI (J1, J6). | First release |\n+| P0 | Web/PWA caching, install path, update safety, error recovery | Keep a stable offline-capable version (J7). | First release |\n+| P0 | Original art/audio feedback and quality validation | Receive clear, original feedback on supported targets (J2, J6). | First release |\n+| P1 | Additional locales, refined remapping, content expansion | Broader comfort and longer campaign. | Later, after languages/content scope is approved |\n+| P1 | Optional account-linked backup/sync | Recover across devices with explicit conflict choices. | Later, approval required |\n+| P1 | Multiplayer, player safety, moderation, support and live operations | Play safely with others. | Later, approval required; not implied by single-player game |\n+| P2 | Live events/seasonal content; player-created/shared content | Receive or create continuing content. | Deferred |\n+| P2 | Advanced analytics/monetisation, purchases, catalogue, entitlements/refunds | Optional commercial/personalised experience. | Deferred; commerce approval required |\n \n-J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n+The P0 set deliberately forms a complete useful journey: J1 starts and teaches,\n+J2 proves the core loop, J3 reaches the ending, J4 supports rewards, J5 makes\n+progress durable, and J6\u2013J7 keep it usable and safe on both targets. None\n+depends on identity, remote services, multiplayer, commerce, or deferred\n+content.\n \n-## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n+## Approval gates and unanswered questions\n \n-Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n+- Select technology, repository, engine/framework, vendors, CI/CD, cost model,\n+  browser/device/network matrix, storage quota, and performance budget.\n+- Decide whether the apparently contradictory account, multiplayer, safety,\n+  live-service, and commerce requirements are cancelled, later scope, or a\n+  separately funded product. Define ownership, consent, retention/deletion,\n+  support, and degraded modes before implementation.\n+- Set exact party/content quantities, languages, audio scope, accessibility\n+  configuration depth, age/parental/legal regions, and certification/store\n+  obligations. These are not inferred from the functional reference.\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff --check && test -s projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md && rg -n '\"'^## |''^### |'\"\\\\| P[0-2] \\\\|' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n9:## Product outcome and personas\n24:## Core journeys\n36:## Domain vocabulary and state ownership\n50:## System relationship map\n87:## Accepted domain chapters\n89:### 1. Game shell, session, input, and responsive presentation\n104:### 2. Exploration, town, dungeon, and campaign content\n117:### 3. Narrative, quests, onboarding, and ending\n132:### 4. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n146:### 5. Inventory, party progression, and economy\n160:### 6. Save, recovery, and offline persistence\n175:### 7. Accessibility, localisation, and audiovisual feedback\n191:### 8. PWA delivery, release safety, diagnostics, and quality operations\n209:## Capability parity and intentional differences\n220:## Prioritised feature catalogue and slices\n224:| P0 | Shell, title/new game, pause, responsive input/presentation | Start and control the game (J1). | First release |\n225:| P0 | One town, one dungeon, original content packaging and validation | Traverse one completable campaign (J3). | First release |\n226:| P0 | Dialogue, quest state, onboarding, short ending | Understand and complete story progress (J1\u2013J3). | First release |\n227:| P0 | Party turn-based combat, tutorial encounter, rewards | Finish first battle and understand the loop (J2). | First release |\n228:| P0 | Inventory/progression UI and safe item rules | Use earned rewards without dead ends (J4). | First release |\n229:| P0 | Atomic local save/load, checkpoints, corruption recovery, save deletion | Stop, resume, and recover confirmed progress (J5). | First release |\n230:| P0 | Settings: accessible controls/text/captions/visuals; localisation pipeline | Play comfortably and read core UI (J1, J6). | First release |\n231:| P0 | Web/PWA caching, install path, update safety, error recovery | Keep a stable offline-capable version (J7). | First release |\n232:| P0 | Original art/audio feedback and quality validation | Receive clear, original feedback on supported targets (J2, J6). | First release |\n233:| P1 | Additional locales, refined remapping, content expansion | Broader comfort and longer campaign. | Later, after languages/content scope is approved |\n234:| P1 | Optional account-linked backup/sync | Recover across devices with explicit conflict choices. | Later, approval required |\n235:| P1 | Multiplayer, player safety, moderation, support and live operations | Play safely with others. | Later, approval required; not implied by single-player game |\n236:| P2 | Live events/seasonal content; player-created/shared content | Receive or create continuing content. | Deferred |\n237:| P2 | Advanced analytics/monetisation, purchases, catalogue, entitlements/refunds | Optional commercial/personalised experience. | Deferred; commerce approval required |\n245:## Approval gates and unanswered questions\n\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 0f5dac970642dc368657cdcea6e66533107d9f80..5fd05892133662659f4e0eb11fd8d2756a222e5e\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,214 +1,255 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n+**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md) and the canonical\n+[project brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md). This document turns only the accepted\n+offline-game scope into a model. It records connected and commercial requests\n+as approval gates; it does not silently turn them into a first-release\n+dependency.\n \n-## Product boundary\n+## Product outcome and personas\n \n-Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n+The first release is successful when a casual or adult player can start the\n+original 2D fantasy adventure, complete an understandable first battle, save,\n+return later without losing confirmed progress, finish the single town and\n+dungeon story, and reach its short ending offline after installation or a\n+successful first asset download.\n \n-The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n-\n-## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n-\n-| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | Success signal | Relevant journeys |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n-| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n-| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n-| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n-\n-Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n+| Casual story player | When I have a short session, help me understand what to do and make visible story progress without complex controls. | Starts, wins the tutorial battle, saves, and resumes a quest. | J1\u2013J4 |\n+| Adult returning player | When I resume after interruption or a long gap, help me recover safely and understand my current objective. | Restored checkpoint is clear; no confirmed progress is silently lost. | J3, J5 |\n+| Player with access needs | When I play on my chosen device, let me read, hear, see, and control the game in a comfortable way. | Can change supported text, caption, visual, and control options before or during play. | J1, J6 |\n+| Offline PWA player | When connectivity changes or an update is available, let me keep playing the installed game without corrupting saves. | Cached release runs offline; update waits for a safe boundary. | J1, J5, J7 |\n \n ## Core journeys\n \n-| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| ID | Journey and outcome | Normal path | Empty / invalid / denied / offline / conflict / recovery / deletion handling |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n-| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n-| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n-| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n-| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n+| J1 | Start, orient, and configure: player reaches the town and knows the tutorial goal. | Open web game or installed PWA \u2192 choose new game \u2192 optional settings \u2192 readable dialogue and input hints \u2192 town. | No prior save shows New Game; unavailable gamepad falls back to listed keyboard/mouse/touch controls; unsupported input shows a non-blocking message. Offline launch is allowed only when the current assets are cached; otherwise explain that an initial download is needed. Declining optional diagnostics does not block play. |\n+| J2 | Learn and win first combat: player understands the turn loop. | Accept first encounter \u2192 choose a legal party action \u2192 resolve enemy turn \u2192 win \u2192 receive reward and quest update. | Empty inventory/action lists show explanation; invalid/stale target or unavailable item is rejected without consuming a turn; defeated party reloads the latest safe checkpoint and explains the consequence. No network permission is needed. |\n+| J3 | Explore and advance the campaign: player finishes town, dungeon, and ending. | Talk to characters \u2192 accept quest \u2192 traverse town/dungeon \u2192 resolve required encounter and objective \u2192 claim reward \u2192 see ending. | Locked exits state the missing quest condition; exhausted dialogue has a safe closing line; missing authored reference fails content validation and shows recoverable error rather than breaking state; optional reward absence never blocks completion. |\n+| J4 | Manage items and progression: player can use earned resources correctly. | Open inventory \u2192 inspect item \u2192 use/equip only where legal \u2192 see immediate feedback \u2192 close back to play. | Empty inventory has an explicit empty state; invalid use, full/limited capacity (if authored), or ineligible target preserves inventory; quest items cannot be discarded if that would make a required path impossible. Deletion/discard is not a first-release capability unless an authored safe rule is later approved. |\n+| J5 | Save, load, and recover: player can leave and return safely. | Manual save or checkpoint completes atomically \u2192 resume chooses latest valid save \u2192 state restores at a safe scene boundary. | No save slot shows explanation; corrupt/incompatible candidate is quarantined, never overwritten, and falls back to the last valid checkpoint or new game; storage denial/quota exposes retry and export/support guidance where feasible; interrupted write keeps prior confirmed save. Local cache and any future remote copy conflict resolve only with explicit player choice; remote sync is not first release. Deleting a save requires confirmation and removes only that selected local save. |\n+| J6 | Adjust accessibility and feedback: player can make the game usable. | Open settings \u2192 preview/change text, captions, visual and input options \u2192 apply \u2192 preferences persist. | Unsupported option/device is labelled unavailable; reset asks confirmation; settings storage failure leaves current-session setting active and explains it may not persist. No permission denial is required for these settings. |\n+| J7 | Install, update, and recover from a fault: player retains a coherent playable version. | Install when browser allows \u2192 cache approved release \u2192 play offline \u2192 accept update at launch/menu after save boundary. | Installation prompt may be unavailable/declined without blocking web play; offline uncached assets state the requirement; cache refresh failure retains known-good cache; crash/error boundary offers retry, return to title, and safe recovery. Diagnostics are minimal and consent-gated where required; a player can decline or delete local diagnostic data without losing play. |\n \n-## Domain vocabulary\n+## Domain vocabulary and state ownership\n \n-| Term | Meaning and rule |\n+| Term | Meaning / owner |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n-| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n-| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n-| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n-| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n-| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n-| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n-| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n-| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n-| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n-| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n-| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n+| Campaign | The fixed first-release sequence from new game through ending; owns completion state. |\n+| Scene | One authored playable location: town or dungeon; owns exits, encounter zones, and presentation references. |\n+| Quest | Authored objective and state machine that gates campaign progress and rewards. |\n+| Party | Player-controlled combat group; owns members' current combat-ready values and progression. Exact party size is an authoring decision. |\n+| Encounter | A combat instance created from an authored encounter definition; never persists mid-turn. |\n+| Item | Authored definition; an inventory stack/reference is the player-owned instance/count. |\n+| Save snapshot | Versioned, validated, atomic representation of durable player state at a safe boundary. |\n+| Checkpoint | Authored or system-selected safe snapshot point; it is not a promise to restore arbitrary moment-to-moment state. |\n+| Content package | Versioned original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, translations, and audiovisual assets delivered together. |\n \n-## System relationship map and invariants\n+## System relationship map\n \n ```text\n-Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n-                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n-Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n-Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n-Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n-All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n-PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n-Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n-Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n-accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n+Content package \u2500\u2500validates/defines\u2500\u2500> Scene \u2500\u2500starts\u2500\u2500> Encounter\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502                  \u2502\n+      \u251c\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Dialogue \u2500\u2500updates\u2500\u2500> Quest <\u2500\u2500rewards\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502       \u2502\n+      \u2514\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Items \u2500\u2500held by\u2500\u2500> Inventory \u2502\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500drives\u2500\u2500> Game shell \u2500\u2500> exploration / UI\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Quest + inventory + party + scene \u2500\u2500snapshot\u2500\u2500> Save / checkpoint\n+Save version <\u2500\u2500compatible-with\u2500\u2500 Content package / PWA release\n+PWA cache + release workflow \u2500\u2500protect\u2500\u2500> runnable assets and save boundary\n+Error recovery + minimal diagnostics \u2500\u2500observe\u2500\u2500> shell, persistence, cache\n ```\n \n-Cross-domain invariants:\n+**Cross-domain invariants and lifecycle boundaries**\n \n-1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n-2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n-3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n-4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n-5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n-6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n-\n-Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n-\n-## Domain chapters\n-\n-### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n-\n-### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n-\n-### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n-\n-### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n-\n-### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n-\n-### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n-\n-### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n-\n-### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+- A quest transition, reward grant, and save snapshot are ordered: validate\n+  transition, apply reward once, then checkpoint; retrying cannot duplicate a\n+  reward.\n+- An encounter may read party, item, and quest state but changes durable state\n+  only after a resolved turn/encounter safe boundary. A crash cannot restore a\n+  half-consumed item or half-applied reward.\n+- A scene exit is available only when its authored quest predicate is true.\n+  Content validation ensures every required predicate, target, reward, and\n+  localisation key exists.\n+- Settings affect presentation/input immediately but never campaign rules.\n+  The same semantic action must be available through supported keyboard,\n+  mouse, touch, and gamepad adaptations.\n+- Save snapshots record content/schema version and are written atomically.\n+  Cache replacement and application migration happen only outside combat and\n+  only while a valid fallback save remains intact.\n+- The local offline campaign neither needs identity nor sends player state.\n+  Any later account is an optional replication boundary, not campaign truth.\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+## Accepted domain chapters\n \n-### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+### 1. Game shell, session, input, and responsive presentation\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Session` moves `launching \u2192 title \u2192 new-or-resume \u2192\n+playing \u2194 paused \u2192 ending \u2192 title`; `InputAction` is mapped per supported input\n+method; `ViewportProfile` selects responsive layout. Authoring inputs are title\n+copy, control hints, menu routes, and UI layouts. Rules: pause blocks world and\n+combat advancement; focus loss pauses or reaches a safe state; UI action labels\n+remain readable and semantic across screen sizes. Dependencies are accessibility,\n+rendering, persistence, and content. Outcome is an understandable, controllable\n+start and return path. Edge cases are no existing save, focus loss, rotation,\n+unavailable gamepad, unsupported browser feature, and input remap conflict.\n+**First-release boundary:** web/PWA keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad\n+adaptation; one shared game core. No native-client shell or arbitrary controller\n+configuration guarantee.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+### 2. Exploration, town, dungeon, and campaign content\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Scene` is `unloaded \u2192 entered \u2192 active \u2192 exited`;\n+`Exit` is `locked | available | used`; `Interactable` is `available \u2192 used` or\n+reusable as authored. Inputs are original tile/layout data, collision shapes,\n+spawn points, NPCs, exits, encounter zones, dialogue, and asset references.\n+Rules: exactly one town and one dungeon form the first campaign; collision and\n+gates must leave a completable route. Dependencies are narrative, quests,\n+combat, rendering, and saves. Outcome is readable navigation to the ending.\n+Edge cases: blocked exit, empty interaction, absent optional item, and invalid\n+content reference. **Boundary:** no extra regions, procedural worlds, or shared\n+levels.\n \n-### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+### 3. Narrative, quests, onboarding, and ending\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Dialogue` is `available \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged`;\n+`Quest` is `unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 accepted \u2192 objective-complete \u2192\n+claimed \u2192 completed` (or `failed` only where authored); `TutorialStep` is\n+`pending \u2192 presented \u2192 understood`; `Campaign` is `not-started \u2192 active \u2192\n+completed`. Inputs are original characters, text, localisation keys, objective\n+predicates, rewards, and ending sequence. Rules: tutorial teaches a battle and\n+saving before unrestricted progression; required quests cannot be abandoned;\n+completion is idempotent. Dependencies are scenes, combat, inventory, UI, and\n+saves. Outcomes are clear objectives and a short finish. Edge cases: repeated\n+NPC talk, dialogue after completion, missing translation, and an invalid quest\n+predicate. **Boundary:** one authored story path; no branching campaign,\n+seasonal narrative, or user-authored stories.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+### 4. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Encounter` is `pending \u2192 active \u2192 player-turn \u2192\n+resolution \u2192 won | lost | escaped-if-authored`; `Combatant` is `ready \u2192 acting\n+\u2192 resolved \u2192 defeated`; `Reward` is `pending \u2192 granted`. Inputs are party and\n+enemy definitions, actions, targeting, encounter placement, reward tables, and\n+balance values. Rules: only legal actions/targets resolve; turn order is visible;\n+reward grants once; defeat returns to a safe checkpoint. Dependencies are party,\n+inventory, quest transitions, audiovisual feedback, and saves. Outcome is a\n+learnable turn-based first battle and reliable progress. Edge cases: no legal\n+target, empty item list, invalid action request, pause/focus loss, crash during\n+resolution, and stale UI selection. **Boundary:** authored encounters only; no\n+multiplayer, ranked modes, live balance changes, or mid-combat save guarantee.\n \n-### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+### 5. Inventory, party progression, and economy\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `InventoryEntry` is `absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 usable |\n+equipped \u2192 consumed-if-applicable`; `PartyMember` progresses through authored\n+states; `RewardGrant` is `unclaimed \u2192 applied`. Inputs are item definitions,\n+descriptions, icons, use/equip restrictions, and rewards. Rules: use validates\n+target and eligibility before mutation; required quest items remain protected;\n+duplicate grants are ignored by stable reward ID. Dependencies are combat,\n+quests, UI, content validation, and saves. Outcome is meaningful reward use\n+without dead ends. Edge cases: empty inventory, ineligible target, capacity\n+limit if selected, duplicate reward, and missing item definition. **Boundary:**\n+small authored set; no crafting, trading, loot boxes, purchases, or destructive\n+discard flow without approval.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n+### 6. Save, recovery, and offline persistence\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `SaveSlot` is `empty \u2192 writing \u2192 valid \u2192 loading` or\n+`quarantined`; `Checkpoint` is `eligible \u2192 committed`; recovery is `detect \u2192\n+preserve \u2192 offer-fallback \u2192 restored | new-game`. Inputs are schema version,\n+campaign/quest/party/inventory/settings state, checkpoint triggers, and\n+migration rules. Rules: validate before replace; retain last known-good data;\n+never overwrite corruption; loading restores only safe scene boundaries; delete\n+requires explicit confirmation. Dependencies are every durable domain, PWA\n+storage, error UX, and release versioning. Outcome is confidence to stop and\n+resume. Edge cases: quota/permission denial, malformed data, interrupted write,\n+old schema, unavailable local storage, offline launch, and later remote conflict.\n+**Boundary:** local durable saves and recovery; remote accounts, sync, cross-\n+device merge, and cloud deletion policy await approval.\n \n-### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n+### 7. Accessibility, localisation, and audiovisual feedback\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Preference` is `default \u2192 previewed \u2192 applied \u2192\n+persisted`; `LocalePack` is `validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active`; `FeedbackEvent`\n+is emitted by action/scene/combat state. Inputs are accessible labels, text\n+styles, captions, visual settings, translations, original art/audio, and event\n+bindings. Rules: settings are understandable and reversible; captions/text do\n+not convey essential information only through sound/colour; missing translation\n+falls back to the approved base language and is logged for content repair.\n+Dependencies are UI, content packaging, input, and storage. Outcome is readable,\n+controllable play with clear feedback. Edge cases: unsupported preference,\n+storage failure, missing locale key, muted audio, contrast conflict, and narrow\n+viewport. **Boundary:** the settings categories named in the brief and a\n+language-content pipeline; exact languages, remapping depth, ratings, and audio\n+production scope remain decisions.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n+### 8. PWA delivery, release safety, diagnostics, and quality operations\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Release` is `built \u2192 validated \u2192 published \u2192 cached \u2192\n+superseded`; `Cache` is `installing \u2192 ready \u2192 retained | replaced`; `Fault` is\n+`caught \u2192 player-recovered \u2192 optionally-reported`. Inputs are asset manifests,\n+content/save compatibility declarations, service-worker policy, error messages,\n+minimal diagnostic schema, and test matrices. Rules: an update activates at a\n+title/menu safe boundary after a confirmed save; failed refresh retains a\n+known-good cache; diagnostics minimise data and respect consent; supported\n+browser/device/network and performance thresholds must be agreed before release\n+acceptance. Dependencies are all packaged content, persistence, shell, privacy,\n+and quality checks. Outcome is safe installation, offline continuation, update,\n+and error recovery. Edge cases: declined install, first offline visit, stale\n+cache, asset fetch failure, crash loop, diagnostics denial/deletion, and storage\n+pressure. **Boundary:** installable web/PWA and minimal crash recovery only; no\n+live event operation, account support desk, analytics programme, or paid-service\n+SLO until approved.\n \n-## Prioritised feature catalogue\n+## Capability parity and intentional differences\n \n-| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n+| Capability | Responsive web | Installed PWA | Shared rule |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n+| Campaign, combat, inventory, quests, settings, saves | Required | Required | Same content, rules, save schema, and accessible semantic actions. |\n+| Input | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad | Same where device/browser exposes it | Input adapters map to one action vocabulary. |\n+| Responsive presentation | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Layout adapts; no gameplay advantage by surface. |\n+| Offline play/assets | May work once cached | Primary expected offline route once cached | First visit needs assets; saves remain local and recoverable. |\n+| Installation | Browser page can offer it where supported | Installable experience | Declining/unavailable install never blocks web play. |\n+| Updates | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Never swap content/version during unresolved combat or save write. |\n \n-## Cross-target contract\n+## Prioritised feature catalogue and slices\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n+| Priority | Feature | User outcome / journey | Slice |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n-| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n-| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n-| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n-| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n-| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness proof\n+| P0 | Shell, title/new game, pause, responsive input/presentation | Start and control the game (J1). | First release |\n+| P0 | One town, one dungeon, original content packaging and validation | Traverse one completable campaign (J3). | First release |\n+| P0 | Dialogue, quest state, onboarding, short ending | Understand and complete story progress (J1\u2013J3). | First release |\n+| P0 | Party turn-based combat, tutorial encounter, rewards | Finish first battle and understand the loop (J2). | First release |\n+| P0 | Inventory/progression UI and safe item rules | Use earned rewards without dead ends (J4). | First release |\n+| P0 | Atomic local save/load, checkpoints, corruption recovery, save deletion | Stop, resume, and recover confirmed progress (J5). | First release |\n+| P0 | Settings: accessible controls/text/captions/visuals; localisation pipeline | Play comfortably and read core UI (J1, J6). | First release |\n+| P0 | Web/PWA caching, install path, update safety, error recovery | Keep a stable offline-capable version (J7). | First release |\n+| P0 | Original art/audio feedback and quality validation | Receive clear, original feedback on supported targets (J2, J6). | First release |\n+| P1 | Additional locales, refined remapping, content expansion | Broader comfort and longer campaign. | Later, after languages/content scope is approved |\n+| P1 | Optional account-linked backup/sync | Recover across devices with explicit conflict choices. | Later, approval required |\n+| P1 | Multiplayer, player safety, moderation, support and live operations | Play safely with others. | Later, approval required; not implied by single-player game |\n+| P2 | Live events/seasonal content; player-created/shared content | Receive or create continuing content. | Deferred |\n+| P2 | Advanced analytics/monetisation, purchases, catalogue, entitlements/refunds | Optional commercial/personalised experience. | Deferred; commerce approval required |\n \n-J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n+The P0 set deliberately forms a complete useful journey: J1 starts and teaches,\n+J2 proves the core loop, J3 reaches the ending, J4 supports rewards, J5 makes\n+progress durable, and J6\u2013J7 keep it usable and safe on both targets. None\n+depends on identity, remote services, multiplayer, commerce, or deferred\n+content.\n \n-## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n+## Approval gates and unanswered questions\n \n-Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n+- Select technology, repository, engine/framework, vendors, CI/CD, cost model,\n+  browser/device/network matrix, storage quota, and performance budget.\n+- Decide whether the apparently contradictory account, multiplayer, safety,\n+  live-service, and commerce requirements are cancelled, later scope, or a\n+  separately funded product. Define ownership, consent, retention/deletion,\n+  support, and degraded modes before implementation.\n+- Set exact party/content quantities, languages, audio scope, accessibility\n+  configuration depth, age/parental/legal regions, and certification/store\n+  obligations. These are not inferred from the functional reference.\n\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 0f5dac970642dc368657cdcea6e66533107d9f80..5fd05892133662659f4e0eb11fd8d2756a222e5e\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,214 +1,255 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n+**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md) and the canonical\n+[project brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md). This document turns only the accepted\n+offline-game scope into a model. It records connected and commercial requests\n+as approval gates; it does not silently turn them into a first-release\n+dependency.\n \n-## Product boundary\n+## Product outcome and personas\n \n-Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n+The first release is successful when a casual or adult player can start the\n+original 2D fantasy adventure, complete an understandable first battle, save,\n+return later without losing confirmed progress, finish the single town and\n+dungeon story, and reach its short ending offline after installation or a\n+successful first asset download.\n \n-The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n-\n-## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n-\n-| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | Success signal | Relevant journeys |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n-| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n-| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n-| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n-\n-Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n+| Casual story player | When I have a short session, help me understand what to do and make visible story progress without complex controls. | Starts, wins the tutorial battle, saves, and resumes a quest. | J1\u2013J4 |\n+| Adult returning player | When I resume after interruption or a long gap, help me recover safely and understand my current objective. | Restored checkpoint is clear; no confirmed progress is silently lost. | J3, J5 |\n+| Player with access needs | When I play on my chosen device, let me read, hear, see, and control the game in a comfortable way. | Can change supported text, caption, visual, and control options before or during play. | J1, J6 |\n+| Offline PWA player | When connectivity changes or an update is available, let me keep playing the installed game without corrupting saves. | Cached release runs offline; update waits for a safe boundary. | J1, J5, J7 |\n \n ## Core journeys\n \n-| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| ID | Journey and outcome | Normal path | Empty / invalid / denied / offline / conflict / recovery / deletion handling |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n-| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n-| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n-| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n-| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n+| J1 | Start, orient, and configure: player reaches the town and knows the tutorial goal. | Open web game or installed PWA \u2192 choose new game \u2192 optional settings \u2192 readable dialogue and input hints \u2192 town. | No prior save shows New Game; unavailable gamepad falls back to listed keyboard/mouse/touch controls; unsupported input shows a non-blocking message. Offline launch is allowed only when the current assets are cached; otherwise explain that an initial download is needed. Declining optional diagnostics does not block play. |\n+| J2 | Learn and win first combat: player understands the turn loop. | Accept first encounter \u2192 choose a legal party action \u2192 resolve enemy turn \u2192 win \u2192 receive reward and quest update. | Empty inventory/action lists show explanation; invalid/stale target or unavailable item is rejected without consuming a turn; defeated party reloads the latest safe checkpoint and explains the consequence. No network permission is needed. |\n+| J3 | Explore and advance the campaign: player finishes town, dungeon, and ending. | Talk to characters \u2192 accept quest \u2192 traverse town/dungeon \u2192 resolve required encounter and objective \u2192 claim reward \u2192 see ending. | Locked exits state the missing quest condition; exhausted dialogue has a safe closing line; missing authored reference fails content validation and shows recoverable error rather than breaking state; optional reward absence never blocks completion. |\n+| J4 | Manage items and progression: player can use earned resources correctly. | Open inventory \u2192 inspect item \u2192 use/equip only where legal \u2192 see immediate feedback \u2192 close back to play. | Empty inventory has an explicit empty state; invalid use, full/limited capacity (if authored), or ineligible target preserves inventory; quest items cannot be discarded if that would make a required path impossible. Deletion/discard is not a first-release capability unless an authored safe rule is later approved. |\n+| J5 | Save, load, and recover: player can leave and return safely. | Manual save or checkpoint completes atomically \u2192 resume chooses latest valid save \u2192 state restores at a safe scene boundary. | No save slot shows explanation; corrupt/incompatible candidate is quarantined, never overwritten, and falls back to the last valid checkpoint or new game; storage denial/quota exposes retry and export/support guidance where feasible; interrupted write keeps prior confirmed save. Local cache and any future remote copy conflict resolve only with explicit player choice; remote sync is not first release. Deleting a save requires confirmation and removes only that selected local save. |\n+| J6 | Adjust accessibility and feedback: player can make the game usable. | Open settings \u2192 preview/change text, captions, visual and input options \u2192 apply \u2192 preferences persist. | Unsupported option/device is labelled unavailable; reset asks confirmation; settings storage failure leaves current-session setting active and explains it may not persist. No permission denial is required for these settings. |\n+| J7 | Install, update, and recover from a fault: player retains a coherent playable version. | Install when browser allows \u2192 cache approved release \u2192 play offline \u2192 accept update at launch/menu after save boundary. | Installation prompt may be unavailable/declined without blocking web play; offline uncached assets state the requirement; cache refresh failure retains known-good cache; crash/error boundary offers retry, return to title, and safe recovery. Diagnostics are minimal and consent-gated where required; a player can decline or delete local diagnostic data without losing play. |\n \n-## Domain vocabulary\n+## Domain vocabulary and state ownership\n \n-| Term | Meaning and rule |\n+| Term | Meaning / owner |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n-| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n-| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n-| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n-| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n-| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n-| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n-| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n-| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n-| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n-| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n-| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n+| Campaign | The fixed first-release sequence from new game through ending; owns completion state. |\n+| Scene | One authored playable location: town or dungeon; owns exits, encounter zones, and presentation references. |\n+| Quest | Authored objective and state machine that gates campaign progress and rewards. |\n+| Party | Player-controlled combat group; owns members' current combat-ready values and progression. Exact party size is an authoring decision. |\n+| Encounter | A combat instance created from an authored encounter definition; never persists mid-turn. |\n+| Item | Authored definition; an inventory stack/reference is the player-owned instance/count. |\n+| Save snapshot | Versioned, validated, atomic representation of durable player state at a safe boundary. |\n+| Checkpoint | Authored or system-selected safe snapshot point; it is not a promise to restore arbitrary moment-to-moment state. |\n+| Content package | Versioned original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, translations, and audiovisual assets delivered together. |\n \n-## System relationship map and invariants\n+## System relationship map\n \n ```text\n-Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n-                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n-Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n-Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n-Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n-All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n-PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n-Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n-Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n-accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n+Content package \u2500\u2500validates/defines\u2500\u2500> Scene \u2500\u2500starts\u2500\u2500> Encounter\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502                  \u2502\n+      \u251c\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Dialogue \u2500\u2500updates\u2500\u2500> Quest <\u2500\u2500rewards\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502       \u2502\n+      \u2514\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Items \u2500\u2500held by\u2500\u2500> Inventory \u2502\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500drives\u2500\u2500> Game shell \u2500\u2500> exploration / UI\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Quest + inventory + party + scene \u2500\u2500snapshot\u2500\u2500> Save / checkpoint\n+Save version <\u2500\u2500compatible-with\u2500\u2500 Content package / PWA release\n+PWA cache + release workflow \u2500\u2500protect\u2500\u2500> runnable assets and save boundary\n+Error recovery + minimal diagnostics \u2500\u2500observe\u2500\u2500> shell, persistence, cache\n ```\n \n-Cross-domain invariants:\n+**Cross-domain invariants and lifecycle boundaries**\n \n-1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n-2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n-3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n-4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n-5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n-6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n-\n-Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n-\n-## Domain chapters\n-\n-### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n-\n-### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n-\n-### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n-\n-### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n-\n-### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n-\n-### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n-\n-### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n-\n-### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+- A quest transition, reward grant, and save snapshot are ordered: validate\n+  transition, apply reward once, then checkpoint; retrying cannot duplicate a\n+  reward.\n+- An encounter may read party, item, and quest state but changes durable state\n+  only after a resolved turn/encounter safe boundary. A crash cannot restore a\n+  half-consumed item or half-applied reward.\n+- A scene exit is available only when its authored quest predicate is true.\n+  Content validation ensures every required predicate, target, reward, and\n+  localisation key exists.\n+- Settings affect presentation/input immediately but never campaign rules.\n+  The same semantic action must be available through supported keyboard,\n+  mouse, touch, and gamepad adaptations.\n+- Save snapshots record content/schema version and are written atomically.\n+  Cache replacement and application migration happen only outside combat and\n+  only while a valid fallback save remains intact.\n+- The local offline campaign neither needs identity nor sends player state.\n+  Any later account is an optional replication boundary, not campaign truth.\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+## Accepted domain chapters\n \n-### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+### 1. Game shell, session, input, and responsive presentation\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Session` moves `launching \u2192 title \u2192 new-or-resume \u2192\n+playing \u2194 paused \u2192 ending \u2192 title`; `InputAction` is mapped per supported input\n+method; `ViewportProfile` selects responsive layout. Authoring inputs are title\n+copy, control hints, menu routes, and UI layouts. Rules: pause blocks world and\n+combat advancement; focus loss pauses or reaches a safe state; UI action labels\n+remain readable and semantic across screen sizes. Dependencies are accessibility,\n+rendering, persistence, and content. Outcome is an understandable, controllable\n+start and return path. Edge cases are no existing save, focus loss, rotation,\n+unavailable gamepad, unsupported browser feature, and input remap conflict.\n+**First-release boundary:** web/PWA keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad\n+adaptation; one shared game core. No native-client shell or arbitrary controller\n+configuration guarantee.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+### 2. Exploration, town, dungeon, and campaign content\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Scene` is `unloaded \u2192 entered \u2192 active \u2192 exited`;\n+`Exit` is `locked | available | used`; `Interactable` is `available \u2192 used` or\n+reusable as authored. Inputs are original tile/layout data, collision shapes,\n+spawn points, NPCs, exits, encounter zones, dialogue, and asset references.\n+Rules: exactly one town and one dungeon form the first campaign; collision and\n+gates must leave a completable route. Dependencies are narrative, quests,\n+combat, rendering, and saves. Outcome is readable navigation to the ending.\n+Edge cases: blocked exit, empty interaction, absent optional item, and invalid\n+content reference. **Boundary:** no extra regions, procedural worlds, or shared\n+levels.\n \n-### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+### 3. Narrative, quests, onboarding, and ending\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Dialogue` is `available \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged`;\n+`Quest` is `unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 accepted \u2192 objective-complete \u2192\n+claimed \u2192 completed` (or `failed` only where authored); `TutorialStep` is\n+`pending \u2192 presented \u2192 understood`; `Campaign` is `not-started \u2192 active \u2192\n+completed`. Inputs are original characters, text, localisation keys, objective\n+predicates, rewards, and ending sequence. Rules: tutorial teaches a battle and\n+saving before unrestricted progression; required quests cannot be abandoned;\n+completion is idempotent. Dependencies are scenes, combat, inventory, UI, and\n+saves. Outcomes are clear objectives and a short finish. Edge cases: repeated\n+NPC talk, dialogue after completion, missing translation, and an invalid quest\n+predicate. **Boundary:** one authored story path; no branching campaign,\n+seasonal narrative, or user-authored stories.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+### 4. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Encounter` is `pending \u2192 active \u2192 player-turn \u2192\n+resolution \u2192 won | lost | escaped-if-authored`; `Combatant` is `ready \u2192 acting\n+\u2192 resolved \u2192 defeated`; `Reward` is `pending \u2192 granted`. Inputs are party and\n+enemy definitions, actions, targeting, encounter placement, reward tables, and\n+balance values. Rules: only legal actions/targets resolve; turn order is visible;\n+reward grants once; defeat returns to a safe checkpoint. Dependencies are party,\n+inventory, quest transitions, audiovisual feedback, and saves. Outcome is a\n+learnable turn-based first battle and reliable progress. Edge cases: no legal\n+target, empty item list, invalid action request, pause/focus loss, crash during\n+resolution, and stale UI selection. **Boundary:** authored encounters only; no\n+multiplayer, ranked modes, live balance changes, or mid-combat save guarantee.\n \n-### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+### 5. Inventory, party progression, and economy\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `InventoryEntry` is `absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 usable |\n+equipped \u2192 consumed-if-applicable`; `PartyMember` progresses through authored\n+states; `RewardGrant` is `unclaimed \u2192 applied`. Inputs are item definitions,\n+descriptions, icons, use/equip restrictions, and rewards. Rules: use validates\n+target and eligibility before mutation; required quest items remain protected;\n+duplicate grants are ignored by stable reward ID. Dependencies are combat,\n+quests, UI, content validation, and saves. Outcome is meaningful reward use\n+without dead ends. Edge cases: empty inventory, ineligible target, capacity\n+limit if selected, duplicate reward, and missing item definition. **Boundary:**\n+small authored set; no crafting, trading, loot boxes, purchases, or destructive\n+discard flow without approval.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n+### 6. Save, recovery, and offline persistence\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `SaveSlot` is `empty \u2192 writing \u2192 valid \u2192 loading` or\n+`quarantined`; `Checkpoint` is `eligible \u2192 committed`; recovery is `detect \u2192\n+preserve \u2192 offer-fallback \u2192 restored | new-game`. Inputs are schema version,\n+campaign/quest/party/inventory/settings state, checkpoint triggers, and\n+migration rules. Rules: validate before replace; retain last known-good data;\n+never overwrite corruption; loading restores only safe scene boundaries; delete\n+requires explicit confirmation. Dependencies are every durable domain, PWA\n+storage, error UX, and release versioning. Outcome is confidence to stop and\n+resume. Edge cases: quota/permission denial, malformed data, interrupted write,\n+old schema, unavailable local storage, offline launch, and later remote conflict.\n+**Boundary:** local durable saves and recovery; remote accounts, sync, cross-\n+device merge, and cloud deletion policy await approval.\n \n-### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n+### 7. Accessibility, localisation, and audiovisual feedback\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Preference` is `default \u2192 previewed \u2192 applied \u2192\n+persisted`; `LocalePack` is `validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active`; `FeedbackEvent`\n+is emitted by action/scene/combat state. Inputs are accessible labels, text\n+styles, captions, visual settings, translations, original art/audio, and event\n+bindings. Rules: settings are understandable and reversible; captions/text do\n+not convey essential information only through sound/colour; missing translation\n+falls back to the approved base language and is logged for content repair.\n+Dependencies are UI, content packaging, input, and storage. Outcome is readable,\n+controllable play with clear feedback. Edge cases: unsupported preference,\n+storage failure, missing locale key, muted audio, contrast conflict, and narrow\n+viewport. **Boundary:** the settings categories named in the brief and a\n+language-content pipeline; exact languages, remapping depth, ratings, and audio\n+production scope remain decisions.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n+### 8. PWA delivery, release safety, diagnostics, and quality operations\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Release` is `built \u2192 validated \u2192 published \u2192 cached \u2192\n+superseded`; `Cache` is `installing \u2192 ready \u2192 retained | replaced`; `Fault` is\n+`caught \u2192 player-recovered \u2192 optionally-reported`. Inputs are asset manifests,\n+content/save compatibility declarations, service-worker policy, error messages,\n+minimal diagnostic schema, and test matrices. Rules: an update activates at a\n+title/menu safe boundary after a confirmed save; failed refresh retains a\n+known-good cache; diagnostics minimise data and respect consent; supported\n+browser/device/network and performance thresholds must be agreed before release\n+acceptance. Dependencies are all packaged content, persistence, shell, privacy,\n+and quality checks. Outcome is safe installation, offline continuation, update,\n+and error recovery. Edge cases: declined install, first offline visit, stale\n+cache, asset fetch failure, crash loop, diagnostics denial/deletion, and storage\n+pressure. **Boundary:** installable web/PWA and minimal crash recovery only; no\n+live event operation, account support desk, analytics programme, or paid-service\n+SLO until approved.\n \n-## Prioritised feature catalogue\n+## Capability parity and intentional differences\n \n-| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n+| Capability | Responsive web | Installed PWA | Shared rule |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n+| Campaign, combat, inventory, quests, settings, saves | Required | Required | Same content, rules, save schema, and accessible semantic actions. |\n+| Input | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad | Same where device/browser exposes it | Input adapters map to one action vocabulary. |\n+| Responsive presentation | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Layout adapts; no gameplay advantage by surface. |\n+| Offline play/assets | May work once cached | Primary expected offline route once cached | First visit needs assets; saves remain local and recoverable. |\n+| Installation | Browser page can offer it where supported | Installable experience | Declining/unavailable install never blocks web play. |\n+| Updates | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Never swap content/version during unresolved combat or save write. |\n \n-## Cross-target contract\n+## Prioritised feature catalogue and slices\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n+| Priority | Feature | User outcome / journey | Slice |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n-| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n-| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n-| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n-| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n-| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n-\n-## First-release completeness proof\n+| P0 | Shell, title/new game, pause, responsive input/presentation | Start and control the game (J1). | First release |\n+| P0 | One town, one dungeon, original content packaging and validation | Traverse one completable campaign (J3). | First release |\n+| P0 | Dialogue, quest state, onboarding, short ending | Understand and complete story progress (J1\u2013J3). | First release |\n+| P0 | Party turn-based combat, tutorial encounter, rewards | Finish first battle and understand the loop (J2). | First release |\n+| P0 | Inventory/progression UI and safe item rules | Use earned rewards without dead ends (J4). | First release |\n+| P0 | Atomic local save/load, checkpoints, corruption recovery, save deletion | Stop, resume, and recover confirmed progress (J5). | First release |\n+| P0 | Settings: accessible controls/text/captions/visuals; localisation pipeline | Play comfortably and read core UI (J1, J6). | First release |\n+| P0 | Web/PWA caching, install path, update safety, error recovery | Keep a stable offline-capable version (J7). | First release |\n+| P0 | Original art/audio feedback and quality validation | Receive clear, original feedback on supported targets (J2, J6). | First release |\n+| P1 | Additional locales, refined remapping, content expansion | Broader comfort and longer campaign. | Later, after languages/content scope is approved |\n+| P1 | Optional account-linked backup/sync | Recover across devices with explicit conflict choices. | Later, approval required |\n+| P1 | Multiplayer, player safety, moderation, support and live operations | Play safely with others. | Later, approval required; not implied by single-player game |\n+| P2 | Live events/seasonal content; player-created/shared content | Receive or create continuing content. | Deferred |\n+| P2 | Advanced analytics/monetisation, purchases, catalogue, entitlements/refunds | Optional commercial/personalised experience. | Deferred; commerce approval required |\n \n-J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n+The P0 set deliberately forms a complete useful journey: J1 starts and teaches,\n+J2 proves the core loop, J3 reaches the ending, J4 supports rewards, J5 makes\n+progress durable, and J6\u2013J7 keep it usable and safe on both targets. None\n+depends on identity, remote services, multiplayer, commerce, or deferred\n+content.\n \n-## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n+## Approval gates and unanswered questions\n \n-Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n+- Select technology, repository, engine/framework, vendors, CI/CD, cost model,\n+  browser/device/network matrix, storage quota, and performance budget.\n+- Decide whether the apparently contradictory account, multiplayer, safety,\n+  live-service, and commerce requirements are cancelled, later scope, or a\n+  separately funded product. Define ownership, consent, retention/deletion,\n+  support, and degraded modes before implementation.\n+- Set exact party/content quantities, languages, audio scope, accessibility\n+  configuration depth, age/parental/legal regions, and certification/store\n+  obligations. These are not inferred from the functional reference.\n\ncodex\n{\"status\":\"pass\",\"summary\":\"Created the complete F002 feature-model specification for the offline 2D RPG web/PWA release.\",\"changed_files\":[\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\"],\"test_result\":\"Passed: `git diff --check`; `test -s projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md`; heading and priority catalogue presence check with `rg`.\",\"evidence\":{\"agent_log\":\"Used F001 discovery and product brief to define only accepted offline scope; unresolved connected/commercial requirements remain approval gates.\",\"adapter_log\":\"\",\"test_log\":\"All focused documentation checks completed successfully from /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator.\",\"browser_log\":\"\",\"screenshots\":[]},\"acceptance_results\":[{\"criterion\":\"Every feature maps to a user outcome and journey.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"The prioritised catalogue maps every feature to J1\u2013J7, and the core-journeys table defines each outcome.\"},{\"criterion\":\"Normal, empty, invalid, permission-denied, offline, conflict, recovery, and deletion paths are specified where relevant.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"The J1\u2013J7 journey table and save/PWA chapters explicitly define these paths where applicable.\"},{\"criterion\":\"The first release provides one complete useful journey without deferred dependencies.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"The P0 release statement explains the complete J1\u2013J7 path and explicitly excludes identity, remote services, multiplayer, commerce, and deferred content.\"},{\"criterion\":\"Cross-target capability parity and intentional target-specific differences are documented.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"The capability parity table covers responsive web and installed PWA, including input, offline behaviour, installation, updates, and shared rules.\"},{\"criterion\":\"Each domain chapter defines its entities, rules, lifecycle, authoring inputs, dependencies, player/user outcomes, edge cases, and first-release boundary.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"Eight accepted-domain chapters consistently provide the requested model dimensions.\"},{\"criterion\":\"The relationship map identifies cross-domain invariants and lifecycle boundaries. For example, an RPG quest links narrative and save state; an IDE edit links language analysis, workspace indexing, and version control; an OS permission links identity, process, storage, and user settings.\",\"status\":\"pass\",\"evidence\":\"The relationship map and invariant list link content, scenes, quests, combat, inventory, input/accessibility, saves, releases, caches, and recovery.\"}],\"documentation\":{\"reviewed_files\":[\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/00-domain-discovery.md\",\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/01-product-brief.md\"],\"updated_files\":[\"projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\"],\"summary\":\"Added the requested user-facing specification; no README change was needed because this is a project specification artifact, not a setup or behavioural change.\"},\"known_limitations\":[\"Technology, vendor, target-support thresholds, content quantities, localisation scope, and connected/commercial requirements remain explicit approval gates from F001.\"],\"browser_coverage\":\"Not applicable: this is domain documentation only; no browser-facing product behaviour changed and no Playwright spec was required.\",\"recommended_next_step\":\"complete\"}\ndiff --git a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\nindex 0f5dac970642dc368657cdcea6e66533107d9f80..5fd05892133662659f4e0eb11fd8d2756a222e5e\n--- a/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n+++ b/jakowicz/runbook-generator/projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/02-feature-model.md\n@@ -1,214 +1,255 @@\n # 02 \u2014 Feature model and release slices\n \n-**Status:** Product specification derived from [00-domain-discovery.md](00-domain-discovery.md). It describes the smallest complete offline campaign; it does not select an engine, backend, vendor, commercial model, or connected-service policy.\n+**Inputs:** [domain discovery](00-domain-discovery.md) and the canonical\n+[project brief](../PROJECT_BRIEF.md). This document turns only the accepted\n+offline-game scope into a model. It records connected and commercial requests\n+as approval gates; it does not silently turn them into a first-release\n+dependency.\n \n-## Product boundary\n+## Product outcome and personas\n \n-Mock Fantasy Quest 4 is an original, small, single-player 2D fantasy RPG for a responsive public web application and installable PWA. A player explores one town and one dungeon, completes a short quest chain, wins turn-based encounters, manages a small inventory, saves or resumes safely, and reaches a short ending. The first release is useful without sign-in, remote sync, multiplayer, purchases, live operations, analytics, seasonal content, or player-created content.\n+The first release is successful when a casual or adult player can start the\n+original 2D fantasy adventure, complete an understandable first battle, save,\n+return later without losing confirmed progress, finish the single town and\n+dungeon story, and reach its short ending offline after installation or a\n+successful first asset download.\n \n-The reference permits only the broad *party-based turn-based adventure* scope. All names, writing, art, audio, UI, maps, rules expression, and interactions are original.\n-\n-## Personas, jobs, and success measures\n-\n-| Persona | Job to be done | First-session success | Product obligations |\n+| Persona | Job to be done | Success signal | Relevant journeys |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Casual story player | \u201cWhen I have a short break, let me make clear progress in a friendly fantasy story without needing genre expertise.\u201d | Completes the first battle and can explain how to save. | Clear objective, low-friction controls, readable state, forgiving recovery. |\n-| Adult returning player | \u201cWhen I return after an interruption or a long gap, let me safely continue from meaningful progress.\u201d | Resumes a valid save and knows what changed since the last checkpoint. | Explicit save status, deterministic resume, pause, recovery and update messaging. |\n-| Access-needs player | \u201cWhen I play with my preferred input and readability settings, let me understand and operate every required action.\u201d | Enables a relevant setting and finishes the tutorial battle. | Keyboard, mouse, touch and gamepad support; captions; readable text; visual controls; no timing-critical required input. |\n-| Offline or unreliable-network player | \u201cWhen the network is absent or weak, let me play the installed campaign and protect my progress.\u201d | Starts from cached content and saves locally. | Offline assets, durable local save attempts, clear disconnected state, no required online dependency. |\n-\n-Outcome measures for independent validation are: a new player can finish the tutorial battle and create/load a save; a returning player can continue at the latest confirmed checkpoint; and every release-one critical path is available on supported screen sizes and input modes when offline after a successful install/cache.\n+| Casual story player | When I have a short session, help me understand what to do and make visible story progress without complex controls. | Starts, wins the tutorial battle, saves, and resumes a quest. | J1\u2013J4 |\n+| Adult returning player | When I resume after interruption or a long gap, help me recover safely and understand my current objective. | Restored checkpoint is clear; no confirmed progress is silently lost. | J3, J5 |\n+| Player with access needs | When I play on my chosen device, let me read, hear, see, and control the game in a comfortable way. | Can change supported text, caption, visual, and control options before or during play. | J1, J6 |\n+| Offline PWA player | When connectivity changes or an update is available, let me keep playing the installed game without corrupting saves. | Cached release runs offline; update waits for a safe boundary. | J1, J5, J7 |\n \n ## Core journeys\n \n-| Journey | Trigger and happy path | Completion / user outcome | Required exceptional paths |\n+| ID | Journey and outcome | Normal path | Empty / invalid / denied / offline / conflict / recovery / deletion handling |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| J1: First useful session | Open game \u2192 choose New Game \u2192 tutorial dialogue \u2192 move \u2192 initiate first encounter \u2192 choose actions \u2192 win \u2192 tutorial save prompt \u2192 save confirmation. | Player has won once and knows how to save. | Empty slot list; invalid/unsupported input is ignored with a visible hint; storage denied/full shows recovery choices; offline works from cached build; crash restores the last confirmed checkpoint. |\n-| J2: Campaign progression | Resume/New \u2192 town quest briefing \u2192 explore \u2192 acquire/use item \u2192 enter dungeon \u2192 win encounters \u2192 complete quest objective \u2192 return/continue \u2192 ending. | Player reaches the authored short ending with recorded completion. | Blocked route gives an intelligible quest hint; empty inventory and unavailable actions are explained; loss returns to a defined checkpoint without corrupting quest state. |\n-| J3: Save, resume, and recovery | Pause/menu \u2192 Save \u2192 confirmation \u2192 close/interruption \u2192 Load/Resume \u2192 validate snapshot \u2192 restore scene, party, quests, inventory, settings. | Safe continuation at a known point. | No saves offers New Game; malformed/incompatible save is quarantined and recovery starts from last valid checkpoint; deletion requires confirmation and is irreversible locally; simultaneous local/remote conflict is out of release-one because remote state is not a dependency. |\n-| J4: Settings and accessibility | Settings \u2192 adjust text/captions/visual/input/audio options \u2192 preview/apply \u2192 resume play. | Player can make required content usable without losing campaign state. | Invalid value is rejected and previous applied value remains; unavailable gamepad/permission is non-blocking; preferences persist separately from save slots; reset requires confirmation. |\n-| J5: Install, offline, and update | Browser visit \u2192 explain Install where available \u2192 install \u2192 cache a complete approved version \u2192 launch offline \u2192 receive update \u2192 choose safe refresh at a non-combat boundary. | Installed player can play offline and avoid losing an active session during update. | Install unavailable is explained; partial/failed cache preserves prior working version; offline first visit gives a recovery page; update never swaps assets mid-session; cache/storage failure directs player to protect/export evidence where feasible and retry. |\n+| J1 | Start, orient, and configure: player reaches the town and knows the tutorial goal. | Open web game or installed PWA \u2192 choose new game \u2192 optional settings \u2192 readable dialogue and input hints \u2192 town. | No prior save shows New Game; unavailable gamepad falls back to listed keyboard/mouse/touch controls; unsupported input shows a non-blocking message. Offline launch is allowed only when the current assets are cached; otherwise explain that an initial download is needed. Declining optional diagnostics does not block play. |\n+| J2 | Learn and win first combat: player understands the turn loop. | Accept first encounter \u2192 choose a legal party action \u2192 resolve enemy turn \u2192 win \u2192 receive reward and quest update. | Empty inventory/action lists show explanation; invalid/stale target or unavailable item is rejected without consuming a turn; defeated party reloads the latest safe checkpoint and explains the consequence. No network permission is needed. |\n+| J3 | Explore and advance the campaign: player finishes town, dungeon, and ending. | Talk to characters \u2192 accept quest \u2192 traverse town/dungeon \u2192 resolve required encounter and objective \u2192 claim reward \u2192 see ending. | Locked exits state the missing quest condition; exhausted dialogue has a safe closing line; missing authored reference fails content validation and shows recoverable error rather than breaking state; optional reward absence never blocks completion. |\n+| J4 | Manage items and progression: player can use earned resources correctly. | Open inventory \u2192 inspect item \u2192 use/equip only where legal \u2192 see immediate feedback \u2192 close back to play. | Empty inventory has an explicit empty state; invalid use, full/limited capacity (if authored), or ineligible target preserves inventory; quest items cannot be discarded if that would make a required path impossible. Deletion/discard is not a first-release capability unless an authored safe rule is later approved. |\n+| J5 | Save, load, and recover: player can leave and return safely. | Manual save or checkpoint completes atomically \u2192 resume chooses latest valid save \u2192 state restores at a safe scene boundary. | No save slot shows explanation; corrupt/incompatible candidate is quarantined, never overwritten, and falls back to the last valid checkpoint or new game; storage denial/quota exposes retry and export/support guidance where feasible; interrupted write keeps prior confirmed save. Local cache and any future remote copy conflict resolve only with explicit player choice; remote sync is not first release. Deleting a save requires confirmation and removes only that selected local save. |\n+| J6 | Adjust accessibility and feedback: player can make the game usable. | Open settings \u2192 preview/change text, captions, visual and input options \u2192 apply \u2192 preferences persist. | Unsupported option/device is labelled unavailable; reset asks confirmation; settings storage failure leaves current-session setting active and explains it may not persist. No permission denial is required for these settings. |\n+| J7 | Install, update, and recover from a fault: player retains a coherent playable version. | Install when browser allows \u2192 cache approved release \u2192 play offline \u2192 accept update at launch/menu after save boundary. | Installation prompt may be unavailable/declined without blocking web play; offline uncached assets state the requirement; cache refresh failure retains known-good cache; crash/error boundary offers retry, return to title, and safe recovery. Diagnostics are minimal and consent-gated where required; a player can decline or delete local diagnostic data without losing play. |\n \n-## Domain vocabulary\n+## Domain vocabulary and state ownership\n \n-| Term | Meaning and rule |\n+| Term | Meaning / owner |\n | --- | --- |\n-| Campaign | The one authored town-to-dungeon story path, including completion state. |\n-| Session | In-memory play between launch/resume and exit; it is never proof that progress is saved. |\n-| Save slot | A named local persisted snapshot plus version/checksum metadata and a confirmed timestamp. |\n-| Checkpoint | An authored safe restoration point. Autosave creates only at approved checkpoints or other non-destructive boundaries. |\n-| Party member | A controllable combatant. Release one may have a small authored party; its number is a content decision, not a feature dependency. |\n-| Encounter | A bounded turn-based battle instantiated from authored enemy and scene data. |\n-| Turn | One legal action selection and resolution cycle; no real-time speed is required. |\n-| Quest | An authored objective with prerequisites, state, reward, and completion consequence. |\n-| Item | An authored inventory object; stack/use/equipment behavior is explicit in its definition. |\n-| Content pack | Versioned original maps, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, art, audio and localisation keys delivered with a release. |\n-| Confirmed save | A snapshot reported written and integrity-checked by the persistence layer. |\n-| Recovery record | Minimal diagnostic context for an interrupted session or failed save; it is not a replacement for a confirmed save. |\n+| Campaign | The fixed first-release sequence from new game through ending; owns completion state. |\n+| Scene | One authored playable location: town or dungeon; owns exits, encounter zones, and presentation references. |\n+| Quest | Authored objective and state machine that gates campaign progress and rewards. |\n+| Party | Player-controlled combat group; owns members' current combat-ready values and progression. Exact party size is an authoring decision. |\n+| Encounter | A combat instance created from an authored encounter definition; never persists mid-turn. |\n+| Item | Authored definition; an inventory stack/reference is the player-owned instance/count. |\n+| Save snapshot | Versioned, validated, atomic representation of durable player state at a safe boundary. |\n+| Checkpoint | Authored or system-selected safe snapshot point; it is not a promise to restore arbitrary moment-to-moment state. |\n+| Content package | Versioned original scenes, dialogue, quests, encounters, items, translations, and audiovisual assets delivered together. |\n \n-## System relationship map and invariants\n+## System relationship map\n \n ```text\n-Content authoring/validation \u2500\u2500> Content pack \u2500\u2500> exploration, narrative, quests,\n-                                          combat, inventory, rendering and localisation\n-Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500> shell/navigation \u2500\u2500> exploration/combat/dialogue/settings\n-Exploration \u2500\u2500> encounter \u2500\u2500> combat \u2500\u2500> rewards \u2500\u2500> inventory + quest progression\n-Narrative \u2500\u2500> quest transitions \u2500\u2500> route/ending availability\n-All durable campaign state \u2500\u2500> checkpoint/save \u2500\u2500> load/recovery \u2500\u2500> shell session\n-PWA cache/update \u2500\u2500> approved content pack + shell; it never mutates an active session\n-Crash boundary \u2500\u2500> recovery record + player recovery UI \u2500\u2500> last confirmed save\n-Privacy/security + quality/release operations constrain diagnostics, storage, cache,\n-accessibility, compatibility and recovery across every arrow.\n+Content package \u2500\u2500validates/defines\u2500\u2500> Scene \u2500\u2500starts\u2500\u2500> Encounter\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502                  \u2502\n+      \u251c\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Dialogue \u2500\u2500updates\u2500\u2500> Quest <\u2500\u2500rewards\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n+      \u2502                                  \u2502       \u2502\n+      \u2514\u2500\u2500defines\u2500\u2500> Items \u2500\u2500held by\u2500\u2500> Inventory \u2502\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Input + accessibility \u2500\u2500drives\u2500\u2500> Game shell \u2500\u2500> exploration / UI\n+                                                 \u2502\n+Quest + inventory + party + scene \u2500\u2500snapshot\u2500\u2500> Save / checkpoint\n+Save version <\u2500\u2500compatible-with\u2500\u2500 Content package / PWA release\n+PWA cache + release workflow \u2500\u2500protect\u2500\u2500> runnable assets and save boundary\n+Error recovery + minimal diagnostics \u2500\u2500observe\u2500\u2500> shell, persistence, cache\n ```\n \n-Cross-domain invariants:\n+**Cross-domain invariants and lifecycle boundaries**\n \n-1. A quest transition, inventory reward, defeated encounter, route unlock, and ending flag become durable together only in a confirmed save/checkpoint; a failed write leaves the previous confirmed save loadable.\n-2. An encounter consumes only an immutable authored definition and a session instance. Its reward is granted exactly once, recorded with the encounter/quest state, and cannot reappear after load.\n-3. Content identifiers, localisation keys, and save schema versions are stable within a released content pack. A load either migrates explicitly or fails safely without overwriting the source slot.\n-4. Settings and accessibility preferences apply to menus and gameplay but cannot change combat legality, quest completion, or save integrity.\n-5. Offline play depends only on local approved assets and local storage. Connected/account features, if later approved, cannot make the campaign, a save, or recovery unavailable.\n-6. Refresh/install/cache transitions occur only at shell-controlled safe boundaries; active combat and unsaved state are preserved or the player is warned before leaving.\n-\n-Lifecycle boundaries are: authored data becomes immutable release content at packaging; content plus user choices becomes ephemeral session state at launch; only validated checkpoints cross into persisted save state; a loaded save becomes a new session only after schema/content validation; obsolete caches and deleted saves are removed only after their respective safety/confirmation rules.\n-\n-## Domain chapters\n-\n-### 1. Game shell, lifecycle, and session recovery\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `LaunchState` moves from `starting` to `title`, `loading-save`, `playing`, `paused`, `recovering`, `error`, or `exiting`. `Session` is created from New Game or validated Load, is marked dirty after state mutation, and ends only after a safe checkpoint/save attempt or explicit abandonment warning. `RecoveryRecord` is created after an unexpected error and expires after successful resume/dismissal.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** The shell owns title, pause, navigation, focus/interruption handling, and safe-boundary checks. It consumes settings, save metadata, content version, input events, and platform install/update status; it depends on persistence, rendering, diagnostics, and PWA lifecycle. It gives the player a predictable start, pause, return, and recovery path.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Normal: title to new/resume; empty: no compatible slots; invalid: bad route/state returns to title with an error; permission-denied: storage failure retains a playable unsaved session with warning; offline: no network dependency; conflict: remote conflict is explicitly absent; recovery: reopen last confirmed checkpoint; deletion: abandon session only after warning. Release one excludes profiles, cloud sync, social entry, and remote account gating.\n-\n-### 2. Exploration, scenes, traversal, and collisions\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `Scene` is an authored town or dungeon map; `PlayerAvatar` has location, facing, and interaction context; `Portal` moves a valid avatar between scenes; `Interactable` changes from available to consumed/changed where authored. A scene instance is loaded from content, updated in session, and reconstructed from saved state.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Movement uses normalized keyboard, pointer/touch, and gamepad commands; collision prevents invalid tiles; interactions require a reachable target; dungeon entrance rules are quest-controlled. Inputs include maps, collision layers, portals, triggers and prompt strings. Dependencies are shell, input/accessibility, content, quests, combat triggers, rendering and saves. Outcome: the player can find the objective and understand why a path is unavailable.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty interaction shows no-op feedback; invalid movement never clips through collision; unavailable input has an alternate supported control; offline is unchanged; a late-loaded/map mismatch stops before session mutation and offers retry/title; no remote conflict exists; scene changes checkpoint only at authored safe boundaries; consumed interactables are retained in save state. Release one contains exactly one town and one dungeon, not an overworld, procedural maps, or fast travel.\n-\n-### 3. Narrative, characters, dialogue, and ending\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `DialogueNode` is authored, localised, displayed, acknowledged, and may set an allowed progression signal. `Character` is an authored identity with current dialogue availability. `Ending` is locked, becomes eligible from the final quest state, then viewed and recorded as completed.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Dialogue is skippable/advanceable at player pace, supports captions/text settings, and must not hide required quest facts in audio-only feedback. Authoring inputs are original scripts, portraits, triggers, speaker metadata and localisation keys. It depends on quest state, UI/accessibility, content, and persistence. Outcome: the player understands current purpose and reaches a coherent short ending.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing/empty optional barks do not block; missing required key fails validation before packaging and shows a recoverable fallback in development; invalid trigger cannot grant progression; offline is unchanged; remote conflict is absent; interrupted dialogue restores at the checkpoint-defined position; no player-authored/deleted narrative. Release one excludes branching romance, voice acting requirement, and post-ending chapters.\n-\n-### 4. Quests, progression, rewards, and tutorial\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `QuestDefinition` supplies prerequisites, objectives, states, rewards and hints. `QuestInstance` moves `locked \u2192 available \u2192 active \u2192 ready-to-complete \u2192 completed`, or `failed` only if authored; it never silently regresses. `TutorialStep` moves `pending \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged \u2192 complete` and is persisted so it is not forced repeatedly. `RewardGrant` is pending, applied once, then durably recorded.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Only authored predicates transition objectives. Tutorial sequence teaches movement, a battle and saving without assuming a party size. Inputs: quest graph, trigger IDs, dialogue hooks, encounter results, item rewards. Dependencies: narrative, exploration, combat, inventory, save and UI. Outcome: a clear path from briefing to ending and a first session that demonstrates battle/save.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional quest log is understandable; invalid/out-of-order trigger is rejected; a full inventory uses defined stack/fallback behavior before reward commit; offline is unchanged; connected conflict is not in scope; recovery replays only uncommitted transitions; no release-one quest deletion. Release one is one linear, small campaign; daily quests, shared quests, seasonal goals and quest authoring by players are deferred.\n-\n-### 5. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `EncounterDefinition` is authored and immutable in a pack. `EncounterInstance` moves `pending \u2192 active \u2192 resolving \u2192 won/lost/escaped (if authored) \u2192 rewards-committed`; it is not saveable mid-resolution unless a later rule explicitly permits it. `Combatant` moves `ready \u2192 selected \u2192 acted \u2192 defeated`; `Action` moves `offered \u2192 selected \u2192 validated \u2192 resolved/rejected`.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Combat is turn-based. Legal actions are derived from living combatants, authored abilities, targets and resources; resolution is deterministic from the saved combat/session state if persistence is later allowed. Inputs are enemy/ability/reward/balance definitions. Dependencies are input/accessibility, UI/audio/visual feedback, quests, inventory, shell and saves. Outcome: understandable tactical fights with readable health, turn, target, result, and reward feedback.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty action/target lists give a clear disabled explanation; invalid action/target cannot mutate state; input loss pauses or waits rather than timing out; offline is unchanged; no remote conflict; crash during combat restores the prior confirmed checkpoint, never a half-awarded result; encounters/rewards cannot be deleted by the player. Release one excludes multiplayer, competitive balance, real-time combat, and remote matchmaking.\n-\n-### 6. Inventory, items, equipment/use rules, and economy\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ItemDefinition` is versioned authored content. `InventoryStack` moves absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 changed/equipped/used \u2192 depleted; `EquipmentSlot` is empty or references one legal owned item. `Reward` is pending then atomically granted with its source progression update.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Inventory displays current quantities and legal use/equip affordances. Items obey stack limits, target rules, and consumption effects defined in content. Inputs are item definitions, rewards, shop/interaction definitions if authored. Dependencies are combat, quests, exploration, UI, content and save. Outcome: player can inspect, gain, equip/use, and understand unavailable items.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty inventory is a first-class screen; invalid/equipped-unowned/use-at-full-health attempts do nothing and explain why; storage write failure leaves prior confirmed state; offline unchanged; no account conflict; recovery avoids duplicate grants; delete/discard is excluded unless a later authored need appears. Release one has no commerce, trading, crafting, loot boxes, or entitlement items.\n-\n-### 7. Save/load, checkpoints, migrations, and local persistence\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `SaveSlot` moves empty \u2192 writing \u2192 confirmed \u2192 loading \u2192 confirmed, or `quarantined` on corruption/incompatibility; explicit deletion moves confirmed \u2192 deleting \u2192 deleted. `CheckpointSnapshot` captures coherent campaign, scene, party, quest, inventory, tutorial and needed session state. Preferences are stored separately. `SaveSchemaVersion` is validated/migrated before load.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Write atomically where platform storage allows: serialize, validate/checksum, commit, then update slot metadata. Never replace a confirmed source until replacement is confirmed. Autosave occurs only at safe authored boundaries; manual save is unavailable or clearly deferred during unsafe combat resolution. Inputs are state serializers, storage adapter, schema migrations and quota result. Dependencies: every stateful game domain, shell, PWA storage, privacy and recovery UI. Outcome: player can trust save, resume, and transparent failure handling.\n-\n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty slots offer New Game; invalid/corrupt saves are quarantined with slot-preserving explanation; denied/full storage reports unsaved progress and retry/options; offline requires no server; remote conflict is a later account-policy feature and must not overwrite local state; recovery uses the newest integrity-valid confirmed snapshot; deletion has slot name/date confirmation and cannot be undone. Release one requires local saves and recovery, not cloud sync/export/import.\n-\n-### 8. Rendering, responsive UI, input, and web performance\n-\n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ViewportProfile` is calculated per device and updates on resize/orientation; `InputBinding` is default, overridden, reset, and persisted in preferences; `UIState` opens, focuses, updates, and closes. Renderable scenes are created from content and disposed on safe scene change.\n-\n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Required actions have keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad paths where applicable; focus indicators and semantic labels are retained; responsive layouts preserve dialogue, inventory, quest and progression readability. Inputs are layout rules, action map, assets and device capabilities. Dependencies are shell, accessibility, all gameplay UI, PWA and quality testing. Outcome: the same campaign is usable across phone, tablet and desktop without a separate product.\n+- A quest transition, reward grant, and save snapshot are ordered: validate\n+  transition, apply reward once, then checkpoint; retrying cannot duplicate a\n+  reward.\n+- An encounter may read party, item, and quest state but changes durable state\n+  only after a resolved turn/encounter safe boundary. A crash cannot restore a\n+  half-consumed item or half-applied reward.\n+- A scene exit is available only when its authored quest predicate is true.\n+  Content validation ensures every required predicate, target, reward, and\n+  localisation key exists.\n+- Settings affect presentation/input immediately but never campaign rules.\n+  The same semantic action must be available through supported keyboard,\n+  mouse, touch, and gamepad adaptations.\n+- Save snapshots record content/schema version and are written atomically.\n+  Cache replacement and application migration happen only outside combat and\n+  only while a valid fallback save remains intact.\n+- The local offline campaign neither needs identity nor sends player state.\n+  Any later account is an optional replication boundary, not campaign truth.\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Narrow viewports reflow rather than hide required state; invalid inputs are ignored safely; unavailable gamepad does not block; offline rendering uses local assets; no remote conflict; rendering failure invokes error boundary; users may reset bindings/settings, not delete content. Quantitative browser/device matrix and performance budgets need approval before release, so release one defines behavior and tests once thresholds are selected.\n+## Accepted domain chapters\n \n-### 9. PWA installability, offline assets, cache, and update safety\n+### 1. Game shell, session, input, and responsive presentation\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AppVersion` is staged \u2192 cached \u2192 active \u2192 superseded \u2192 eligible for eviction. `CacheManifest` is validated before activation. `InstallPromptState` is available/unavailable/dismissed/installed. `UpdateState` is none/downloaded/ready/deferred/applied.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Session` moves `launching \u2192 title \u2192 new-or-resume \u2192\n+playing \u2194 paused \u2192 ending \u2192 title`; `InputAction` is mapped per supported input\n+method; `ViewportProfile` selects responsive layout. Authoring inputs are title\n+copy, control hints, menu routes, and UI layouts. Rules: pause blocks world and\n+combat advancement; focus loss pauses or reaches a safe state; UI action labels\n+remain readable and semantic across screen sizes. Dependencies are accessibility,\n+rendering, persistence, and content. Outcome is an understandable, controllable\n+start and return path. Edge cases are no existing save, focus loss, rotation,\n+unavailable gamepad, unsupported browser feature, and input remap conflict.\n+**First-release boundary:** web/PWA keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad\n+adaptation; one shared game core. No native-client shell or arbitrary controller\n+configuration guarantee.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** A successful online visit caches the approved shell and required campaign assets. Activation is atomic at an application safe boundary. The player is told when an update is ready and can defer refresh until a save/exit boundary. Inputs are build manifest, service worker events, cache/storage results and shell session state. Dependencies: release/versioning, save, shell, content pack, diagnostics and quality. Outcome: installable offline play with no surprise session loss.\n+### 2. Exploration, town, dungeon, and campaign content\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Install unavailable is explanatory; cache miss/offline first launch reports what is needed; invalid manifest keeps last good cache; denied/quota-limited cache offers browser-storage guidance; no server conflict; interrupted update preserves a usable prior version; cache deletion follows browser controls and must never delete confirmed saves intentionally. Release one excludes mandatory login, streaming content and live patching during play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Scene` is `unloaded \u2192 entered \u2192 active \u2192 exited`;\n+`Exit` is `locked | available | used`; `Interactable` is `available \u2192 used` or\n+reusable as authored. Inputs are original tile/layout data, collision shapes,\n+spawn points, NPCs, exits, encounter zones, dialogue, and asset references.\n+Rules: exactly one town and one dungeon form the first campaign; collision and\n+gates must leave a completable route. Dependencies are narrative, quests,\n+combat, rendering, and saves. Outcome is readable navigation to the ending.\n+Edge cases: blocked exit, empty interaction, absent optional item, and invalid\n+content reference. **Boundary:** no extra regions, procedural worlds, or shared\n+levels.\n \n-### 10. Accessibility, localisation, audio/visual feedback, and original assets\n+### 3. Narrative, quests, onboarding, and ending\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `AccessibilityPreference` and `LocalePreference` are selected, validated, applied and persisted. `CaptionEvent` is emitted with relevant audio, displayed, then dismissed. `Asset` moves authored \u2192 reviewed/licensed-original \u2192 packaged \u2192 referenced \u2192 retired. `FeedbackEvent` is emitted from game state and presented through permitted visual/audio channels.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Dialogue` is `available \u2192 shown \u2192 acknowledged`;\n+`Quest` is `unavailable \u2192 available \u2192 accepted \u2192 objective-complete \u2192\n+claimed \u2192 completed` (or `failed` only where authored); `TutorialStep` is\n+`pending \u2192 presented \u2192 understood`; `Campaign` is `not-started \u2192 active \u2192\n+completed`. Inputs are original characters, text, localisation keys, objective\n+predicates, rewards, and ending sequence. Rules: tutorial teaches a battle and\n+saving before unrestricted progression; required quests cannot be abandoned;\n+completion is idempotent. Dependencies are scenes, combat, inventory, UI, and\n+saves. Outcomes are clear objectives and a short finish. Edge cases: repeated\n+NPC talk, dialogue after completion, missing translation, and an invalid quest\n+predicate. **Boundary:** one authored story path; no branching campaign,\n+seasonal narrative, or user-authored stories.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Text, captions, contrast/visual options and accessible controls apply in title, dialogue, combat, inventory, quest and settings UI. Required gameplay information has a readable visual/text channel; feedback must be distinguishable without colour or sound alone. Authoring inputs include original art/audio, palette/readability rules, caption text, localisation keys and accessibility acceptance cases. Dependencies: UI, content, input, all gameplay domains and packaging. Outcome: player can perceive and operate the campaign in their chosen configuration.\n+### 4. Party combat, encounters, rewards, and balance\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Missing optional feedback degrades gracefully; missing required caption/key blocks package validation; unsupported locale falls back to the approved default with disclosure; unavailable audio does not remove meaning; offline local assets work; preferences have no remote conflict; reset/delete applies only to preferences after confirmation where material. Languages, voice, exact settings list, and legal accessibility conformance target remain approval decisions; release one must nevertheless include the explicitly required accessibility behaviors.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Encounter` is `pending \u2192 active \u2192 player-turn \u2192\n+resolution \u2192 won | lost | escaped-if-authored`; `Combatant` is `ready \u2192 acting\n+\u2192 resolved \u2192 defeated`; `Reward` is `pending \u2192 granted`. Inputs are party and\n+enemy definitions, actions, targeting, encounter placement, reward tables, and\n+balance values. Rules: only legal actions/targets resolve; turn order is visible;\n+reward grants once; defeat returns to a safe checkpoint. Dependencies are party,\n+inventory, quest transitions, audiovisual feedback, and saves. Outcome is a\n+learnable turn-based first battle and reliable progress. Edge cases: no legal\n+target, empty item list, invalid action request, pause/focus loss, crash during\n+resolution, and stale UI selection. **Boundary:** authored encounters only; no\n+multiplayer, ranked modes, live balance changes, or mid-combat save guarantee.\n \n-### 11. Content authoring, validation, packaging, and level delivery\n+### 5. Inventory, party progression, and economy\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ContentSource` (maps, scripts, quests, encounters, items, assets) moves draft \u2192 reviewed \u2192 validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 released \u2192 retired. `ContentPack` is immutable when released and is identified by version. `ValidationIssue` is open \u2192 fixed/waived; a blocking issue prevents packaging.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `InventoryEntry` is `absent \u2192 acquired \u2192 usable |\n+equipped \u2192 consumed-if-applicable`; `PartyMember` progresses through authored\n+states; `RewardGrant` is `unclaimed \u2192 applied`. Inputs are item definitions,\n+descriptions, icons, use/equip restrictions, and rewards. Rules: use validates\n+target and eligibility before mutation; required quest items remain protected;\n+duplicate grants are ignored by stable reward ID. Dependencies are combat,\n+quests, UI, content validation, and saves. Outcome is meaningful reward use\n+without dead ends. Edge cases: empty inventory, ineligible target, capacity\n+limit if selected, duplicate reward, and missing item definition. **Boundary:**\n+small authored set; no crafting, trading, loot boxes, purchases, or destructive\n+discard flow without approval.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Validators check stable IDs, references, collision/portal reachability, quest graph completeness, localisation keys, legal combat actions, reward uniqueness, asset presence, original-content provenance and save compatibility declarations. Dependencies are every gameplay domain, localisation/assets, PWA cache, release operations and quality. Outcome: the small town/dungeon campaign ships as coherent original content rather than unverified loose data.\n+### 6. Save, recovery, and offline persistence\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** Empty optional collections are permitted only where a rule says so; invalid references/duplicate IDs block release; tool permission failure preserves sources and reports remediation; offline runtime consumes already packaged content; content-version/save incompatibility requires migration or safe load refusal; deletion/retirement waits until no supported save/update path needs it. Release one needs a controlled internal authoring pipeline, not player modding, shared levels, or a public creator portal.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `SaveSlot` is `empty \u2192 writing \u2192 valid \u2192 loading` or\n+`quarantined`; `Checkpoint` is `eligible \u2192 committed`; recovery is `detect \u2192\n+preserve \u2192 offer-fallback \u2192 restored | new-game`. Inputs are schema version,\n+campaign/quest/party/inventory/settings state, checkpoint triggers, and\n+migration rules. Rules: validate before replace; retain last known-good data;\n+never overwrite corruption; loading restores only safe scene boundaries; delete\n+requires explicit confirmation. Dependencies are every durable domain, PWA\n+storage, error UX, and release versioning. Outcome is confidence to stop and\n+resume. Edge cases: quota/permission denial, malformed data, interrupted write,\n+old schema, unavailable local storage, offline launch, and later remote conflict.\n+**Boundary:** local durable saves and recovery; remote accounts, sync, cross-\n+device merge, and cloud deletion policy await approval.\n \n-### 12. Release operations, diagnostics, privacy/security, and quality\n+### 7. Accessibility, localisation, and audiovisual feedback\n \n-**Entities and lifecycle.** `ReleaseCandidate` moves built \u2192 validated \u2192 approved \u2192 published \u2192 supported/rolled-back; `DiagnosticEvent` is created \u2192 minimised/scrubbed \u2192 queued/sent or discarded; `Incident` is detected \u2192 triaged \u2192 mitigated \u2192 resolved; `TestCase` is authored \u2192 run \u2192 passed/failed. No player account entity is part of release-one play.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Preference` is `default \u2192 previewed \u2192 applied \u2192\n+persisted`; `LocalePack` is `validated \u2192 packaged \u2192 active`; `FeedbackEvent`\n+is emitted by action/scene/combat state. Inputs are accessible labels, text\n+styles, captions, visual settings, translations, original art/audio, and event\n+bindings. Rules: settings are understandable and reversible; captions/text do\n+not convey essential information only through sound/colour; missing translation\n+falls back to the approved base language and is logged for content repair.\n+Dependencies are UI, content packaging, input, and storage. Outcome is readable,\n+controllable play with clear feedback. Edge cases: unsupported preference,\n+storage failure, missing locale key, muted audio, contrast conflict, and narrow\n+viewport. **Boundary:** the settings categories named in the brief and a\n+language-content pipeline; exact languages, remapping depth, ratings, and audio\n+production scope remain decisions.\n \n-**Rules, inputs, dependencies, and outcome.** Error boundaries create player-readable recovery choices and minimal crash context. Diagnostics need notice/consent and retention/deletion policy before collection; until then, local player-facing reporting/recovery is required but remote reporting is an approval gate. Quality covers campaign progression, save corruption/recovery, offline/cache/update, responsive inputs, accessibility, content validation and browser performance once targets are selected. Dependencies are all domains and release tooling. Outcome: a player gets a safe recovery path and a release is validated against its promises.\n+### 8. PWA delivery, release safety, diagnostics, and quality operations\n \n-**Edges and release boundary.** No diagnostic data is a valid state; malformed events are discarded; denied consent disables non-essential telemetry; offline queues only where approved or discards safely; no remote account conflict; recovery never exposes personal data; deletion/retention requests require a later data-controller policy. Live status, on-call, support SLAs, analytics, multiplayer moderation, account identity, purchases/refunds and seasonal operations are not first-release dependencies and require decisions.\n+**Entities/lifecycle:** `Release` is `built \u2192 validated \u2192 published \u2192 cached \u2192\n+superseded`; `Cache` is `installing \u2192 ready \u2192 retained | replaced`; `Fault` is\n+`caught \u2192 player-recovered \u2192 optionally-reported`. Inputs are asset manifests,\n+content/save compatibility declarations, service-worker policy, error messages,\n+minimal diagnostic schema, and test matrices. Rules: an update activates at a\n+title/menu safe boundary after a confirmed save; failed refresh retains a\n+known-good cache; diagnostics minimise data and respect consent; supported\n+browser/device/network and performance thresholds must be agreed before release\n+acceptance. Dependencies are all packaged content, persistence, shell, privacy,\n+and quality checks. Outcome is safe installation, offline continuation, update,\n+and error recovery. Edge cases: declined install, first offline visit, stale\n+cache, asset fetch failure, crash loop, diagnostics denial/deletion, and storage\n+pressure. **Boundary:** installable web/PWA and minimal crash recovery only; no\n+live event operation, account support desk, analytics programme, or paid-service\n+SLO until approved.\n \n-## Prioritised feature catalogue\n+## Capability parity and intentional differences\n \n-| Priority / slice | Feature | Maps to outcome and journey | Dependencies / boundary |\n+| Capability | Responsive web | Installed PWA | Shared rule |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Title, new game, pause, session recovery UI | Start/return safely; J1, J2, J3 | Shell + local save; no account requirement. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | One town, one dungeon, collision, interaction and route gating | Progress through campaign; J1, J2 | Authored content, quests, rendering. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Readable original dialogue, characters, quest log and short ending | Understand and finish story; J1, J2 | Narrative, localisation-ready content, saves. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Tutorial and linear quest/progression chain | Learn battle/save and know next goal; J1, J2 | Combat, inventory, narrative. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Turn-based encounters, results and feedback | Win first battle and advance; J1, J2 | Input, accessibility, authored balance. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Inventory, authored items, use/equipment rules and rewards | Manage progression tools; J2 | Combat/quest atomic reward rules. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Local manual/checkpoint save, load, migration guard and recovery | Resume without data loss; J1, J3 | All durable state; no cloud dependency. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Responsive web UI and keyboard/mouse/touch/gamepad support | Play on chosen device/control; J1\u2013J4 | Accessibility, browser test matrix pending approval. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Settings: readable text, captions, visual/audio and accessible controls | Configure usable experience; J4 | Separate preferences persistence. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Installable PWA, offline asset cache and safe update prompt | Play offline without unsafe refresh; J5 | Versioned content, cache tests. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Original asset/content pipeline and validation | Receive coherent original campaign; J1, J2, J5 | All authored content. |\n-| P0 \u2014 first release | Player-facing error boundary and local recovery | Recover after failure; J3, J5 | Diagnostics/privacy policy gates remote reporting. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Optional account identity and remote save sync/conflict UX | Continue across devices; extension of J3 | Requires identity, privacy, conflict and support policy; cannot replace local saves. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Remote crash reporting, support triage and status operations | Obtain support after failures | Requires consent, retention, vendor and operational ownership. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Multiplayer and player-safety services | Play with others safely | Contradicts current single-player scope until mode/moderation policy is approved. |\n-| P1 \u2014 later, approval required | Purchases and entitlements | Optional paid content | Requires commerce, refund, region and compliance decisions. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Seasonal/live events | Return for new timed content | Requires live-service operations; no first-release dependency. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Player-created/shared content | Create and share levels/stories | Requires creator tools, hosting, moderation and safety. |\n-| P2 \u2014 deferred | Advanced analytics/monetisation | Improve/monetise service | Explicitly deferred; privacy policy required. |\n+| Campaign, combat, inventory, quests, settings, saves | Required | Required | Same content, rules, save schema, and accessible semantic actions. |\n+| Input | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad | Same where device/browser exposes it | Input adapters map to one action vocabulary. |\n+| Responsive presentation | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Required on phone, tablet, desktop | Layout adapts; no gameplay advantage by surface. |\n+| Offline play/assets | May work once cached | Primary expected offline route once cached | First visit needs assets; saves remain local and recoverable. |\n+| Installation | Browser page can offer it where supported | Installable experience | Declining/unavailable install never blocks web play. |\n+| Updates | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Cache refresh at safe boundary | Never swap content/version during unresolved combat or save write. |\n \n-## Cross-target contract\n+## Prioritised feature catalogue and slices\n \n-| Capability | Responsive public web application | PWA | Parity / intentional difference |\n+| Priority | Feature | User outcome / journey | Slice |\n | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n-| Core campaign, saves, settings and accessibility | Required in browser | Required in installed or browser PWA context | Same shared game state and content. |\n-| Inputs and responsive layout | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad; phone/tablet/desktop layout | Same when platform/browser exposes each input | Adapter differs by device capability; no gameplay advantage. |\n-| Offline | Available after required assets are cached; first online visit may be needed | Primary installed offline experience after successful cache | Same game behavior; PWA provides install entry and managed cache lifecycle. |\n-| Save storage | Browser local storage mechanism subject to quota/permissions | Same local storage behavior subject to quota/permissions | Same slot semantics/recovery; no remote sync assumed. |\n-| Updates | Safe refresh notice in browser session | Service-worker/cache update staged for safe boundary | PWA additionally manages cache activation/install; neither interrupts active play. |\n-| Install | Browser-specific, not guaranteed | Install affordance where platform supports it | Unavailable installation is never a blocker to play. |\n-\n-#