n \n-J1 is a complete useful journey with no deferred dependency: an anonymous player opens cached or online original content, starts locally, learns movement and turn-based battle, wins, and receives a confirmed local save. J2 then connects the same local systems through the single town and dungeon to the ending. J3\u2013J5 protect access, readability, interruption, offline use, and safe updates. Account, cloud conflict resolution, multiplayer, commerce, live operations, and advanced analytics add no prerequisite to this chain.\n+The P0 set deliberately forms a complete useful journey: J1 starts and teaches,\n+J2 proves the core loop, J3 reaches the ending, J4 supports rewards, J5 makes\n+progress durable, and J6\u2013J7 keep it usable and safe on both targets. None\n+depends on identity, remote services, multiplayer, commerce, or deferred\n+content.\n \n-## Open decisions and explicit non-assumptions\n+## Approval gates and unanswered questions\n \n-Before implementation/release planning, a product owner must select: engine/framework and vendors; exact supported browser/device/network/storage/performance matrix; authored content quantities and party composition; languages and localisation process; precise accessibility settings and conformance target; audio production scope; remote crash-reporting consent/retention/data-controller policy; account/sync conflict policy; multiplayer/safety scope; commerce; live-service ownership; and age/region/legal/store requirements. These are not silently resolved here.\n+- Select technology, repository, engine/framework, vendors, CI/CD, cost model,\n+  browser/device/network matrix, storage quota, and performance budget.\n+- Decide whether the apparently contradictory account, multiplayer, safety,\n+  live-service, and commerce requirements are cancelled, later scope, or a\n+  separately funded product. Define ownership, consent, retention/deletion,\n+  support, and degraded modes before implementation.\n+- Set exact party/conte